var e=JSON.parse(`[[2401,"The key attribute helps React identify the elements in a list on an update. Without keys React will recreate every element, which reduces the performance. With a unique key React can determine exactly which elements have changed, been added or been deleted, and update only those. That makes the reconciliation process more efficient.","The role of key in the reconciliation process\\nIn React the reconciliation process is responsible for comparing the virtual DOM with the real one and applying the minimal changes. When React updates a list of elements, it uses the key attribute in order to identify every element. Without keys React will compare the elements by index, which can lead to an incorrect update and to the loss of state.\\n\\nHow this works\\nWhen rendering a list, React creates a virtual representation. When the data changes, React compares the old and the new virtual lists. If the elements have unique keys, React can determine quickly which elements have been added, deleted or moved. That lets it minimize the changes in the real DOM.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe practical application\\nUse unique, stable keys (an id from a database, for example). Avoid using the array index if the order of the elements can change. That matters especially for components with state, such as an input or a checkbox.\\n\\nConclusion: the proper use of key improves the performance of React applications and prevents errors with components' state when lists are updated dynamically.","// Bad: without a key, or with the index\\n{items.map((item, index) => <li key={index}>{item.name}</li>)}\\n\\n// Good: with a unique id\\n{items.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}"],[2402,"A custom hook is worth extracting when one and the same logic is repeated in several components. That improves the readability, the testability and the reuse of the code. A hook is also justified if the logic is complex and you want to isolate it from the UI — working with an API, subscriptions, localStorage or forms, for example.","When should logic be moved into a custom hook?\\nCustom hooks are a way of extracting repeated logic out of components. They let you separate the state and the side effects from the presentation, following the principle of the separation of responsibility. The main signal for creating a hook is the duplication of code: if you see the same logic in two or more components, that is a candidate for extraction.\\n\\nExamples of the use\\n\\n- Working with an API (loading the data, handling the errors, the loading state)\\n- Managing forms (validation, submission)\\n- Subscriptions to events (resize, scroll, WebSocket)\\n- Working with localStorage or cookies\\n- Timers and intervals\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nCustom hooks are worth applying when logic with state or effects has to be reused between components. They make the code cleaner and make testing and maintenance easier, especially in large projects.","import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction useWindowWidth() {\\n  const [width, setWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const handleResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);\\n    window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n    return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return width;\\n}\\n\\n// Using it in a component\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  const width = useWindowWidth();\\n  return <div>Window width: {width}</div>;\\n}"],[2403,"Unique keys help React identify every element of a list on an update. Without them React may repaint every element, which reduces the performance. Using the array index as the key can lead to errors when elements are added, deleted or rearranged. A unique, stable key (an id from a database, for example) guarantees that only the elements that have changed are updated properly.","What is key in React for?\\nWhen rendering lists React uses the \`key\` attribute in order to identify every element. That lets the reconciliation algorithm determine efficiently which elements have been added, deleted or changed. Without keys React will repaint the whole list on any change, which affects the performance negatively.\\n\\nWhy are unique ids better than the index?\\nUsing the array index as the key can lead to incorrect behaviour. When an element is added at the start of a list, for example, the indexes of all of the following elements shift, and React may match the old and the new elements wrongly, which will cause errors in the components' state or an unexpected repaint. A unique id that does not depend on the position in the list solves that problem.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nAlways use unique, stable identifiers (an id from a database, for example) as the key for lists in React. That ensures the DOM is updated properly and prevents errors related to components' state.","// Bad: key={index}\\n{items.map((item, index) => <li key={index}>{item.name}</li>)}\\n\\n// Good: key={item.id}\\n{items.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}"],[2404,"A component needs optimizing if it repaints often without its input data changing, causes delays in the interface or consumes a lot of resources. Use the React DevTools Profiler in order to reveal the bottlenecks. Pay attention to redundant re-renders of child components when their props are unchanged.","The signs that optimization is needed\\nThe main signal is a noticeable slowing of the interface when the user interacts with it. A component may repaint too often, even when its props and state are not changing. That is especially critical for large lists, complex forms or animations.\\n\\nThe diagnostic tools\\n\\n- The React DevTools Profiler — it records and shows which components repaint and how long that takes.\\n- The browser's Performance tab — it helps you assess the overall load on the main thread.\\n- Logging by means of \`console.log\` in the component's body — a simple way of seeing the frequency of the calls.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nIn this example \`ExpensiveList\` will repaint on every click, even though \`items\` does not change. Using \`React.memo\` will prevent the redundant re-renders.\\n\\nConclusion\\nOptimization is justified when profiling shows real performance problems. You should not optimize in advance — measure first, then apply \`React.memo\`, \`useMemo\` or \`useCallback\` only to the bottlenecks.","function ExpensiveList({ items }) {\\n  console.log('ExpensiveList rendered');\\n  return items.map(item => <div key={item.id}>{item.name}</div>);\\n}\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const items = useMemo(() => [{ id: 1, name: 'A' }], []);\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Click</button>\\n      <ExpensiveList items={items} />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}"],[2405,"If you do not state the array of dependencies in useEffect, the effect will run after every render of the component. That can lead to an infinite loop if the state is changed inside the effect. To run it only on mounting, you have to pass an empty array []. Stating the dependencies is an important practice for controlling side effects.","What happens without the array of dependencies?\\nWhen you use the \`useEffect\` hook without the second argument (the array of dependencies), the effect will be started after every render of the component. That means that on any change of the state or the props that causes a re-render, the effect will run again.\\n\\nWhy is that a problem?\\nIf you change the state inside such an effect (through \`setState\`, for example), that will cause a new render, which will start the effect again, and so on for ever. That leads to the browser hanging or to errors.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example every render increments \`count\`, which causes a new render and starts the effect again.\\n\\nHow do you state the dependencies properly?\\n\\n- An empty array \`[]\` — the effect runs only on mounting and unmounting.\\n- An array with variables \`[count]\` — the effect runs only when those variables change.\\n- Without an array — the effect runs after every render (use that with care).\\n\\nConclusion\\nAlways state the array of dependencies in \`useEffect\`, in order to control when the side effect should run. That prevents unexpected behaviour and improves the application's performance.","import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  // Without dependencies — an infinite loop\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    setCount(count + 1);\\n  });\\n\\n  return <div>{count}</div>;\\n}"],[2406,"The callback version of setState guarantees you are working with the component's most current state. React can group several setState calls into one, so when using the object form the state can be stale. The callback function receives the previous state and props, which lets you update the state on the basis of its previous value safely. That matters especially on multiple consecutive setState calls or when working with asynchronous operations.","Why is the callback version of setState preferable?\\nIn React the setState method is asynchronous. That means the state is not updated instantly after setState is called. React can group several setState calls into one, in order to optimize the performance. If you use the object form of setState, then on multiple calls you risk obtaining a stale state, since every call will rely on one and the same initial value.\\n\\nHow the callback version works\\nThe callback version of setState takes a function, which receives two arguments: the previous state (prevState) and the current props (props). That guarantees you are always working with current data, even if several setState calls happen consecutively.\\n\\nWhen does this matter especially\\n\\n- On multiple consecutive updates of the state\\n- When working with asynchronous operations, where the state can change between the calls\\n- In loops or event handlers, where setState is called several times\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse the callback version of setState whenever the new state depends on the previous one. That prevents bugs related to stale data and makes the code more predictable. The object form is safe only for the simple cases, where the new state does not depend on the old.","// The problem with the object form\\nthis.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });\\nthis.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });\\n// The result: count will increase by only 1, not by 2\\n\\n// The right solution with a callback\\nthis.setState((prevState) => ({ count: prevState.count + 1 }));\\nthis.setState((prevState) => ({ count: prevState.count + 1 }));\\n// The result: count will increase by 2"],[2407,"If you pass a new function into a memoized component, React.memo will not be able to prevent the re-render, since a new reference to the function is created on every render of the parent. The props are compared by reference, and a new function counts as a change. To solve that, useCallback is used, which stabilizes the reference to the function between renders.","How React.memo works\\nReact.memo is a higher-order component, which remembers the result of a render and skips a repeat render if the props have not changed. The comparison of the props is performed shallowly, by reference. If you pass a new function in, its reference will differ from the previous one, and React.memo will consider that the props have changed, which will lead to a re-render.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nEvery time setCount is called the parent re-renders, a new arrow function is created for onClick, and Child re-renders too, despite React.memo.\\n\\nThe solution with useCallback\\n\\nuseCallback returns one and the same reference to the function until the dependencies change (in this case an empty array). Now React.memo sees that onClick has not changed and skips the re-render of Child.\\n\\nConclusion\\nPassing a new function into a memoized component negates the benefit of the memoization. Use useCallback in order to stabilize the references to the functions passed as props, especially if the component is heavy or re-renders often.","const Child = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <p>{count}</p>\\n      <Child onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)} />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(c => c + 1);\\n  }, []);\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <p>{count}</p>\\n      <Child onClick={handleClick} />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}"],[2408,"An empty array as the initial state in React can cause redundant re-renders, because a new array is created every time the initializer function is called. That breaks the referential identity and can lead to infinite loops in useEffect. It is better to use null or undefined and to create the array only when it is needed.","The problem of an empty array as the initial state\\nIn React, when the \`useState\` hook is used, the initial value is passed once, on the first render. If you pass an empty array \`[]\`, then every time the initializer function is called (on a lazy initialization, for example) a new array object will be created. That breaks the principle of referential identity: React compares states by reference, and a new array always counts as a change.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nAs a result the effect fires endlessly, causing redundant requests or updates. That is especially critical in components with frequent re-renders.\\n\\nThe right solution\\nUse \`null\` or \`undefined\` as the initial state and create the array only when it is needed:\\n\\nOr use lazy initialization with a function that returns the array once:\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn empty array as the initial state in React is an anti-pattern, which can lead to unexpected side effects and reduced performance. Always use neutral values (null, undefined) or lazy initialization, in order to avoid problems with re-renders.","const [items, setItems] = useState([]); // A new array on every render\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  // This effect will run on every render,\\n  // because items is a new reference every time\\n  fetchData();\\n}, [items]); // The items dependency always changes\\n\\nconst [items, setItems] = useState(null);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  if (items === null) {\\n    fetchData().then(data => setItems(data));\\n  }\\n}, [items]);\\n\\nconst [items, setItems] = useState(() => []); // It is called only once"],[2409,"To encapsulate the loading, error and data states, a custom hook is used, which manages the asynchronous request. The hook returns an object with the fields data, loading and error. That lets you reuse the loading logic in different components and avoid duplicating code.","Encapsulating the states of loading data\\nWhen working with asynchronous requests in React, you often have to manage three states: loading, error and the successful data. Encapsulating that logic in a custom hook lets you reuse it in different components, making the code cleaner and more maintainable.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\nLet us create a \`useFetch\` hook, which takes a URL and returns an object with the states:\\n\\nUsing it in a component\\nThe component simply calls the hook and displays the corresponding state:\\n\\nConclusion\\nEncapsulating the loading states in a custom hook makes managing asynchronous data easier and improves the readability and reuse of the code. This approach is recommended in any React application that has requests to an API.","import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction useFetch(url) {\\n  const [data, setData] = useState(null);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\\n  const [error, setError] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const fetchData = async () => {\\n      try {\\n        setLoading(true);\\n        const response = await fetch(url);\\n        if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network error');\\n        const result = await response.json();\\n        setData(result);\\n      } catch (err) {\\n        setError(err.message);\\n      } finally {\\n        setLoading(false);\\n      }\\n    };\\n    fetchData();\\n  }, [url]);\\n\\n  return { data, loading, error };\\n}\\n\\nfunction UserList() {\\n  const { data, loading, error } = useFetch('/api/users');\\n\\n  if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;\\n  if (error) return <div>Error: {error}</div>;\\n  return <ul>{data.map(user => <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>)}</ul>;\\n}"],[2410,"Memoization in React (React.memo, useMemo, useCallback) works on the basis of a shallow comparison. When an object is passed, a new object with a new reference is created every time, so the comparison shows that the object has changed and the memoization does not fire. To solve that, you have to stabilize the references by means of useMemo or useCallback, or pass primitive values.","Why memoization does not work with objects\\nMemoization in React is an optimization technique, which prevents repeat renders or computations if the input data has not changed. It is based on a shallow comparison, however — that is, the references to the objects are compared rather than their content. If a new object is created on every render, its reference will differ from the previous one, and the memoization will consider that the data has changed.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\nLet us look at a component that uses React.memo:\\n\\nEvery time Parent renders, a new data object with a new reference is created. React.memo sees that the data prop has changed (the reference is different) and repaints Child, despite the fact that the object's content is the same.\\n\\nHow do you fix it\\nFor the memoization to work, you have to stabilize the reference to the object. Use useMemo or useCallback for that:\\n\\nNow the data object will be created once and will have the same reference on the subsequent renders, as long as the dependencies do not change.\\n\\nConclusion\\nMemoization with objects requires stable references. Use useMemo for objects and useCallback for functions, in order to avoid redundant renders. If an object depends on the props or the state, include them in the array of dependencies — then the memoization will update properly when the data changes.","const Child = React.memo(({ data }) => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered');\\n  return <div>{data.value}</div>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const data = { value: 'test' }; // a new object on every render\\n  return <Child data={data} />;\\n}\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const data = useMemo(() => ({ value: 'test' }), []);\\n  return <Child data={data} />;\\n}"],[2411,"It is better to pass primitives, since they are compared by value and do not cause redundant re-renders when React.memo is used. Objects are created anew on every render, which breaks the memoization. If an object is needed, it should be stabilized through useMemo or moved outside the component.","The effect of the props' type on the re-renders\\nIn React, on every update of a component all of its child elements repaint by default. For optimization React.memo is used, which compares the new and the old props. Primitives (numbers, strings, booleans) are compared by value — if the value has not changed, the component will not repaint. Objects, however, are compared by reference, and on every render of the parent a new object is created, even if its content is identical.\\n\\nAn example with primitives\\n\\nHere Child will repaint only when count changes.\\n\\nThe problem with objects\\n\\nChild will repaint on every click, even if data has not changed.\\n\\nHow to pass objects properly\\n\\n- Use useMemo in order to stabilize the reference:\\n\\n- Move the object outside the component if it is static:\\n\\n- Split the object into separate primitive props, if that is possible.\\n\\nConclusion: for maximum performance and predictable behaviour of the memoization, it is preferable to pass primitives. If an object is necessary, it has to be stabilized by means of useMemo or moved outside the component.","const Child = React.memo(({ count }) => {\\n  console.log('Child render');\\n  return <div>{count}</div>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>+</button>\\n      <Child count={count} />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nconst Child = React.memo(({ data }) => {\\n  console.log('Child render');\\n  return <div>{data.value}</div>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const data = { value: 'test' }; // a new object every render\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>+</button>\\n      <Child data={data} />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nconst data = useMemo(() => ({ value: 'test' }), []);\\n\\nconst DATA = { value: 'test' };"],[2412,"In useCallback you have to state all of the variables and functions from the outer scope that are used inside the callback. That includes the props, the state and the other values the function's working depends on. If you do not state a dependency, the callback will use a stale value. React itself prompts you through the linter as to which dependencies are needed.","What useCallback is, and what the dependencies are for\\nuseCallback is a React hook, which returns a memoized version of a callback. It prevents a new function being created on every render if the dependencies have not changed. The array of dependencies — the hook's second argument — determines when the callback should be recreated.\\n\\nWhich dependencies to state\\nThe array of dependencies has to include all of the variables, functions, props and pieces of state that are used inside the callback and can change between renders. If a callback uses a value that is not stated in the dependencies, it will capture a stale value from the closure.\\n\\nThe rules and the recommendations\\n\\n- Always state all of the variables used inside the callback\\n- If the callback uses only the setter functions from useState, the dependencies can be omitted, since they are stable\\n- Use eslint-plugin-react-hooks for an automatic check of the dependencies\\n- Do not state in the dependencies functions that are themselves memoized through useCallback or useMemo\\n\\nConclusion\\nStating the dependencies in useCallback properly is critically important for the application to work correctly. Mistakes in the dependencies lead to bugs with stale data and to unexpected behaviour. Always follow the rule: state all of the variables from the outer scope that the callback uses, and use the linter for an automatic check.","import React, { useState, useCallback } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const [step, setStep] = useState(1);\\n\\n  // Right: step is stated in the dependencies\\n  const increment = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(prev => prev + step);\\n  }, [step]);\\n\\n  // Wrong: step is not stated, so the old value will be used\\n  const badIncrement = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(prev => prev + step);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={increment}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2413,"useCallback is needed when you pass a function as a prop into a child component wrapped in React.memo, or when the function is used in the array of dependencies of other hooks (useEffect, useMemo). If the function is created inside the component and passed into an ordinary HTML element (div, button) or into a component without React.memo, useCallback gives no gain, since the re-render will happen anyway. You should also not use useCallback for simple functions called on an event (onClick, onChange) if there are no performance problems.","When is useCallback needed?\\nuseCallback in React is used in order to memoize functions. It returns one and the same reference to the function between renders until the dependencies change. That is useful in two main scenarios:\\n- Passing a function into a child component wrapped in React.memo. React.memo prevents a re-render if the props have not changed, but without useCallback every new reference to the function will count as a change of the prop.\\n- Using a function in the array of dependencies of other hooks (useEffect, useMemo, useCallback). If the function is not memoized, it will be created anew on every render, causing infinite loops or redundant calls of the effects.\\n\\nWhen is useCallback not needed?\\nIf the function is passed into an ordinary HTML element (\`<button onClick={...}>\`, for example), useCallback gives no gain, since the re-render will happen anyway. You should also not use it for simple event handlers if there are no measurable performance problems. Premature optimization can complicate the code.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nIn this example \`ExpensiveChild\` is wrapped in React.memo. Without useCallback every re-render of Parent would create a new function and React.memo would not fire. With useCallback the reference to the function is stable, and the child component does not re-render needlessly.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse useCallback only when there is a real performance problem or when the function takes part in the dependencies of other hooks. Do not apply it to every function indiscriminately — that complicates the code to no benefit.","import React, { useState, useCallback } from 'react';\\n\\nconst ExpensiveChild = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {\\n  console.log('Child re-rendered');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  // Without useCallback: a new function on every render\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n  }, []); // The dependencies are empty, so the function is stable\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <ExpensiveChild onClick={handleClick} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2414,"The structure of components in React should be modular and hierarchical. The components are divided into presentational ones (responsible for the display) and container ones (which manage the logic and the data). The files are grouped by functional module or by feature rather than by type. Every component is held in a separate folder with its own styles, tests and an index.js in order to make importing easier.","The principles on which components are organized in React\\nA proper structure of components is the basis of a scalable React application. The main idea is the separation of responsibility: every component should perform one task. That makes testing, reuse and the maintenance of the code easier.\\n\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Grouping by feature (feature-based): every piece of the application's functionality (authorization, the list of products, for example) has its own folder, inside which lie all of the related components, styles, tests and hooks.\\n- The separation into presentational and container components: the presentational components are responsible only for displaying the UI, receiving the data through props. The container components manage the state and the business logic, passing the data to the presentational ones.\\n- Atomic Design: the components are divided into atoms (buttons, inputs), molecules (forms), organisms (the site's header), templates and pages. That helps you create a single design system.\\n\\nAn example of a folder structure\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Use an index.js for re-exporting a component from its folder — that makes the imports simpler.\\n- Avoid deep nesting (more than 3–4 levels).\\n- Move shared logic into custom hooks (useFetch, for example).\\n- For the styles use CSS Modules or styled-components, in order to avoid conflicts of names.\\n\\nConclusion\\nApply a modular, feature-based structure for medium and large projects. For small applications a simple grouping by type (components, containers, pages) is enough. The main thing is to observe uniformity and the single responsibility principle.","src/\\n  components/          # The common reusable components\\n    Button/\\n      index.js\\n      Button.jsx\\n      Button.module.css\\n      Button.test.js\\n  features/            # The application's features\\n    auth/\\n      components/\\n        LoginForm/\\n          index.js\\n          LoginForm.jsx\\n          LoginForm.module.css\\n      hooks/\\n        useAuth.js\\n      api/\\n        authApi.js\\n  pages/               # The pages (the composition of the features)\\n    HomePage.jsx\\n    ProfilePage.jsx\\n  App.jsx\\n  index.js"],[2415,"Docker is a tool for containerization, which packages an application and its dependencies into an isolated environment. React is a library for creating user interfaces. Docker is not part of React, but it can be used for deploying React applications, ensuring the environment is uniform at the different stages of development and in production.","What Docker is, and how it relates to React\\nDocker is a platform for containerization, which lets you package applications and all of their dependencies (libraries, system utilities, settings) into lightweight, isolated containers. React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces. Docker is not part of React and is not used inside the React code itself. Docker is often applied for deploying React applications, however, in order to guarantee that the application works the same way at every stage: from development to production.\\n\\nWhy use Docker with React?\\nThe main aim is to eliminate the \\"it works on my machine\\" problem. Docker creates a single environment, which includes Node.js, npm, the system dependencies and the application itself. That makes setting things up for new developers, testing in CI/CD and deploying to a server easier.\\n\\nAn example of a Dockerfile for a React application\\n\\nIn this example the React application is first built in a container with Node.js, then the finished static files are copied into an image with Nginx, which will serve the files. That is the standard approach for production.\\n\\nConclusion\\nDocker is not part of React, but it is a powerful tool for the containerization of React applications. It is worth applying when the reproducibility of the environment has to be ensured and the deployment and the integration with CI/CD pipelines made easier.","# We use the official Node.js image for the build\\nFROM node:18-alpine AS build\\nWORKDIR /app\\nCOPY package*.json ./\\nRUN npm install\\nCOPY . .\\nRUN npm run build\\n\\n# We use Nginx to serve the static files\\nFROM nginx:alpine\\nCOPY --from=build /app/build /usr/share/nginx/html\\nEXPOSE 80\\nCMD [\\"nginx\\", \\"-g\\", \\"daemon off;\\"]"],[2416,"SSR (Server-Side Rendering) generates the HTML on the server and sends the finished page to the client. CSR (Client-Side Rendering) sends empty HTML plus JavaScript, which renders the page in the browser. SSR improves SEO and the time of the first load, but it loads the server. CSR is faster after the load and reduces the load on the server, but it is worse for SEO.","The main difference between SSR and CSR\\nSSR (Server-Side Rendering) and CSR (Client-Side Rendering) are two approaches to rendering web pages. In SSR the HTML is generated on the server for every request; in CSR on the client, by means of JavaScript, after the page has loaded.\\n\\nHow SSR works\\nWith SSR the server receives the request, performs the logic needed (requests to a database, for example), forms the complete HTML document and sends it to the browser. The browser displays the finished page at once. An example in Node.js with Express:\\n\\nHow CSR works\\nWith CSR the server sends a minimal HTML file with a link to the JavaScript. The browser downloads the JS, which then renders the content. An example with React:\\n\\nWhen to use which\\n\\n- SSR — for sites with high SEO requirements (online shops, blogs, for example) and in order to improve the time of the first load (the First Contentful Paint).\\n- CSR — for applications with a rich interface (dashboards, admin panels, for example), where interactivity after the load matters.\\n\\nConclusion: SSR is better for content sites, CSR for dynamic applications. A hybrid approach (Next.js, for example) is often used, in order to obtain the advantages of both.","const express = require('express');\\nconst app = express();\\napp.get('/', (req, res) => {\\n  const html = \`<html><body><h1>Hello, SSR!</h1></body></html>\`;\\n  res.send(html);\\n});\\napp.listen(3000);\\n\\n// index.html\\n<html><body><div id=\\"root\\"></div><script src=\\"app.js\\"><\/script></body></html>\\n\\n// app.js\\nimport React from 'react';\\nimport ReactDOM from 'react-dom';\\nReactDOM.render(<h1>Hello, CSR!</h1>, document.getElementById('root'));"],[2417,"Redux Saga is a middleware library for Redux, which manages side effects such as asynchronous requests or access to the store. It uses ES6 generators for writing sagas — functions that listen for actions and perform logic. Sagas make the code more testable and more manageable than thunks.","What is Redux Saga?\\nRedux Saga is a middleware for Redux, intended for managing side effects in applications. It lets you write asynchronous logic such as API calls, timers or access to the store in the form of sagas — generator functions. The sagas listen for dispatched actions and can start, pause or cancel tasks, which makes them a powerful tool for complex data flows.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nRedux Saga uses ES6 generators (functions with \`function*\`) and effects such as \`call\`, \`put\`, \`takeEvery\` and \`takeLatest\`. The effects are simple objects, which the middleware interprets and performs. \`call\` calls an asynchronous function, for example, while \`put\` dispatches a new action to the Redux store.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example the \`watchFetchUser\` saga listens for the \`FETCH_USER_REQUEST\` action and starts \`fetchUser\`, which calls the API and dispatches the success or the failure.\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nRedux Saga is often used in large React applications, where complex management of asynchronous flows is required — when working with WebSockets, with parallel requests or with the cancellation of operations, for example. It is especially useful when side effects have to be tested in isolation.\\n\\nConclusion: Redux Saga is worth applying when the application has many complex asynchronous scenarios that require fine control, cancellation or composition, and when the testability and the readability of the code matter.","import { call, put, takeEvery } from 'redux-saga/effects';\\nimport { fetchUserApi } from './api';\\n\\nfunction* fetchUser(action) {\\n  try {\\n    const user = yield call(fetchUserApi, action.payload);\\n    yield put({ type: 'FETCH_USER_SUCCESS', payload: user });\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    yield put({ type: 'FETCH_USER_FAILURE', payload: error });\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction* watchFetchUser() {\\n  yield takeEvery('FETCH_USER_REQUEST', fetchUser);\\n}"],[2418,"A library of components is a set of reusable UI elements assembled into a separate package. It is normally held in a monorepo or in a separate repository, published to an internal npm registry and documented by means of Storybook. Every component should be isolated, covered by tests and versioned. That speeds development up and maintains the visual uniformity of the products.","What a library of components is\\nA library of components is a centralized set of reusable UI elements (buttons, inputs, modal windows and so on), which are used in all of the company's projects. It is the technical implementation of a design system and lets developers avoid creating the same elements from scratch every time.\\n\\nOrganizing the storage and the versioning\\nMost often the library is placed in a separate repository or in a monorepo (using Nx or Turborepo, for example). Every component is a separate module with its own styles, tests and documentation. The versioning follows semver, and the publication goes to an internal npm registry (Verdaccio, GitHub Packages or JFrog).\\n\\nThe process of developing a component\\nThe development is carried out in isolation by means of tools such as Storybook, which lets you view and test components in different states. Every component should:\\n- Take its settings through props (customization)\\n- Be accessible (a11y)\\n- Have unit tests and visual regression tests (Chromatic, for example)\\n- Contain documentation with examples of the usage\\n\\nAn example of a component's structure\\n\\nThe integration into the projects\\nThe projects include the library as an ordinary npm dependency. When the library's version is updated, the developers receive the new components and fixes. For backward compatibility it matters to follow semver and to publish a changelog.\\n\\nConclusion: an internal library of components is justified when the company has several products or teams and a single visual style is required. It reduces the duplication of code, speeds development up and makes maintaining the UI easier.","// Button/Button.tsx\\nimport React from 'react';\\nimport './Button.css';\\n\\ninterface ButtonProps {\\n  variant: 'primary' | 'secondary';\\n  children: React.ReactNode;\\n  onClick?: () => void;\\n}\\n\\nexport const Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({ variant, children, onClick }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <button className={\`btn btn--\${variant}\`} onClick={onClick}>\\n      {children}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n};"],[2419,"A design system is a set of standards, rules and ready-made components for creating uniform interfaces. It includes UI components, design tokens (colours, fonts), guidelines and documentation. Using a design system speeds development up, makes maintenance easier and ensures the products are consistent. Examples: Material Design, Ant Design.","What is a design system?\\nA design system is a comprehensive set of standards, principles, rules and ready-made components, which ensures the uniformity of the user interfaces across all of a company's products. It is not merely a library of UI components but a coherent system, including design tokens (colours, typography, spacing), patterns of interaction, guidelines on accessibility and documentation.\\n\\nThe main parts\\n\\n- UI components: buttons, inputs, modal windows, tables and so on.\\n- Design tokens: variables for the colours, fonts and spacing (\`--color-primary: #1890ff\`, for example).\\n- Patterns: repeated solutions for typical tasks (navigation, forms, filters).\\n- Documentation: the rules of use, examples of code, best practices.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation in React\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nA design system is used in large projects with several teams of developers and designers. It is critically important for products where a single user experience is required (corporate portals, SaaS platforms, mobile applications, for example).\\n\\nConclusion: a design system is worth adopting when the company has several products or teams, in order to avoid the fragmentation of the interfaces, to speed development up and to make maintaining the code easier.","// An example of using design tokens in CSS-in-JS\\nconst theme = {\\n  colors: {\\n    primary: '#1890ff',\\n    background: '#f0f2f5',\\n  },\\n  spacing: (n) => \`\${n * 8}px\`,\\n};\\n\\n// A button component from the design system\\nconst Button = styled.button\`\\n  background: \${props => props.theme.colors.primary};\\n  padding: \${props => props.theme.spacing(2)} \${props => props.theme.spacing(4)};\\n  border: none;\\n  border-radius: 4px;\\n  color: white;\\n  cursor: pointer;\\n\`;"],[2420,"Validation errors should be displayed next to the corresponding input field and should be comprehensible and specific. Use red text messages, which appear after the form is submitted or when the field loses focus. Avoid general messages at the top of the page, since that makes finding the error harder. The messages should explain what exactly is wrong and how to fix it.","Displaying validation errors in forms\\nValidation errors are the messages that inform the user about incorrectly entered data. Displaying the errors properly is critically important for the usability of a form. The errors should be placed directly next to the field they relate to, so that the user sees the problem at once. The messages should be specific — \\"The password must contain at least 8 characters\\", for example, rather than simply \\"Invalid input\\".\\n\\nThe main principles\\n\\n- The placement: the error is displayed under the input field or next to it, often with a red border around the field.\\n- The time of appearance: the errors can be shown after the form is submitted or in real time during the input (after the focus is lost).\\n- The colour and the icons: use red for the error text and possibly a warning icon, in order to attract attention.\\n- Accessibility: the messages should be available to screen readers, using the aria-describedby attribute or role=\\"alert\\".\\n\\nAn example of the code in React\\n\\nIn this example the errors are displayed under each field after the form is submitted. Such an approach helps the user understand quickly which fields have to be corrected.\\n\\nConclusion\\nDisplaying validation errors properly improves the user experience, reduces the number of mistakes when filling forms in and speeds the interaction up. Use localized, specific messages next to the fields and take accessibility for all users into account.","function Form() {\\n  const [errors, setErrors] = useState({});\\n\\n  const validate = (data) => {\\n    const newErrors = {};\\n    if (!data.email) newErrors.email = 'Email is required';\\n    if (!data.password || data.password.length < 6)\\n      newErrors.password = 'The password must be at least 6 characters';\\n    return newErrors;\\n  };\\n\\n  const handleSubmit = (e) => {\\n    e.preventDefault();\\n    const formData = new FormData(e.target);\\n    const validationErrors = validate({\\n      email: formData.get('email'),\\n      password: formData.get('password'),\\n    });\\n    setErrors(validationErrors);\\n    if (Object.keys(validationErrors).length === 0) {\\n      // sending the data\\n    }\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\\n      <div>\\n        <label>Email</label>\\n        <input name=\\"email\\" type=\\"email\\" />\\n        {errors.email && <span style={{color: 'red'}}>{errors.email}</span>}\\n      </div>\\n      <div>\\n        <label>Password</label>\\n        <input name=\\"password\\" type=\\"password\\" />\\n        {errors.password && <span style={{color: 'red'}}>{errors.password}</span>}\\n      </div>\\n      <button type=\\"submit\\">Send</button>\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n}"],[2421,"React is a JavaScript library for creating user interfaces, based on components. Redux is a tool for managing an application's state, which helps you hold the data centrally and change it predictably. React is responsible for rendering the UI, while Redux is responsible for the logic of working with the data, especially in large applications.","What is React?\\nReact is a JavaScript library developed by Facebook for building user interfaces. It uses a component-based approach, in which every element of the interface is an independent, reusable component. React manages a virtual DOM, which lets it update only the parts of the page that have changed efficiently, ensuring high performance.\\n\\nWhat is Redux?\\nRedux is a predictable state container for JavaScript applications. It helps you manage an application's global state in a single centralized store. The state is changed only through pure functions — the reducers, which take the current state and an action and return the new state. That makes the data flow unidirectional and easy to track.\\n\\nHow do they work together?\\nReact and Redux are often used together, but they solve different tasks. React is responsible for rendering the UI on the basis of the data, while Redux is responsible for holding and changing that data. React components subscribe to parts of the state from the Redux store and receive the updates when it changes. To connect React to Redux, the react-redux library is used, which provides hooks such as useSelector and useDispatch.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact and Redux are a powerful combination for creating complex, scalable web applications. React provides flexibility in building the UI, while Redux provides predictable state management. Use Redux when your application has a lot of shared state that is used in different components, or when strict control over the data flow is required.","// The Redux store\\nimport { createStore } from 'redux';\\n\\nconst initialState = { count: 0 };\\n\\nfunction counterReducer(state = initialState, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT':\\n      return { count: state.count + 1 };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst store = createStore(counterReducer);\\n\\n// A React component\\nimport { useSelector, useDispatch } from 'react-redux';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const count = useSelector((state) => state.count);\\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' })}>+</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2422,"To support SSR on the server, you have to set up a Node.js environment that can run React code. You have to use the ReactDOMServer methods, such as renderToString or renderToPipeableStream, in order to transform the components into HTML. Routing on the server also has to be implemented, in order to handle the requests and deliver the finished HTML. It matters to ensure hydration on the client, so that React can take control of the static HTML.","What is needed for SSR on the server?\\nTo implement Server-Side Rendering (SSR) in a React application, the server has to be able to run the components' JavaScript code and generate HTML. Node.js with Express or another framework is normally used for that. The server has to handle the incoming HTTP requests, determine which component to render, and return a complete HTML document.\\n\\nThe main parts of the server side\\n\\n- A Node.js environment — the server has to support the running of React code (JSX, ES modules).\\n- ReactDOMServer — the React library that provides the methods for rendering on the server: \`renderToString\` (synchronous) or \`renderToPipeableStream\` (streaming).\\n- Routing — the server has to match the URL to the components (by means of React Router on the server, for example).\\n- Hydration — on the client you have to call \`hydrateRoot\` rather than \`createRoot\`, so that React attaches itself to the existing HTML.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nSSR requires the server to be set up to run React code and generate HTML. That improves SEO and the time of the first render, but it increases the load on the server. It is applied in projects where indexing by search engines and the fast loading of the content matter.","import express from 'express';\\nimport React from 'react';\\nimport { renderToString } from 'react-dom/server';\\nimport App from './App';\\n\\nconst app = express();\\n\\napp.get('*', (req, res) => {\\n  const html = renderToString(<App />);\\n  res.send(\`\\n    <!DOCTYPE html>\\n    <html>\\n      <head><title>SSR App</title></head>\\n      <body>\\n        <div id=\\"root\\">\${html}</div>\\n        <script src=\\"/client.js\\"><\/script>\\n      </body>\\n    </html>\\n  \`);\\n});\\n\\napp.listen(3000);"],[2423,"The main difficulties when using Persist in Zustand include problems with the serialization of non-serializable data (functions, Map, Set), conflicts between versions of the stored state, the limit on the size of localStorage, and the need to clear stale data by hand. Problems with the asynchronous loading of the state and with synchronization between the browser's tabs can also arise.","The problems of serialization\\nThe Persist middleware serializes the state into JSON automatically. That means any non-serializable data types, such as functions, Map, Set, Symbol or circular references, will be lost or will cause an error. If a handler function or an instance of a class is held in the state, for example, then on restoration from localStorage that data will be turned into null or will cause an exception.\\n\\nConflicts between versions, and stale data\\nWhen the store's structure changes (fields are added or removed), the old data from localStorage may not match the new schema. That leads to errors or to things working incorrectly. You have to provide a mechanism for the migration or the versioning of the state.\\n\\nThe storage's limits\\nlocalStorage has a limit of about 5–10 MB, depending on the browser. Holding large volumes of data (a cache of images or long lists, for example) can exceed that limit, which will lead to a write error. It is recommended that you hold only the critically important data and use compression or partial persistence.\\n\\nAsynchronous loading and synchronization\\nPersist loads the state asynchronously, which can cause problems with rendering on the server (SSR) or during the application's initialization. The data is also not synchronized between the tabs automatically — if the user has opened the application in two tabs, the changes in one will not be reflected in the other without additional handling.\\n\\nConclusion\\nPersist in Zustand is convenient for preserving state between sessions, but it requires a careful approach to the serialization of the data, to the management of the versions and to the browser storage's limits. Apply it for small volumes of data, avoid holding functions and complex objects, and always provide migrations when the structure of the state changes.","import { create } from 'zustand'\\nimport { persist } from 'zustand/middleware'\\n\\nconst useStore = create(\\n  persist(\\n    (set) => ({\\n      data: new Map([['key', 'value']]), // it is not serialized\\n      handler: () => console.log('test'), // it will be lost\\n    }),\\n    { name: 'my-store' }\\n  )\\n)\\n\\npersist(\\n  (set) => ({\\n    version: 1,\\n    user: { name: 'John' },\\n  }),\\n  {\\n    name: 'user-store',\\n    version: 1,\\n    migrate: (persistedState, version) => {\\n      if (version === 0) {\\n        return { ...persistedState, version: 1 }\\n      }\\n      return persistedState\\n    },\\n  }\\n)"],[2424,"Zustand is a lightweight library for managing state in React. Its main advantages: minimal boilerplate, ease of use, the absence of providers, and high performance thanks to targeted re-renders. The disadvantages: a smaller ecosystem and community than Redux's, limited built-in tools for complex logic (side effects, for example) and possible problems with scaling in very large applications.","What Zustand is, and what it is for\\nZustand is a library for managing state in React, which offers a simple, minimalist API. Unlike Redux, it does not require you to wrap the application in a Provider, and creating a store comes down to a call of the \`create\` function. That makes Zustand attractive for small and medium projects, where the speed of development and a low barrier to entry matter.\\n\\nThe main advantages\\n\\n- Minimal boilerplate: to create a store one call of \`create\` is enough; there is no need to write reducers and actions and to dispatch them.\\n- Simplicity and intuitiveness: the API resembles the ordinary React \`useState\` hook, which makes it easier for beginners to learn.\\n- The absence of providers: there is no need to wrap the application in a \`<Provider>\`, which reduces the nesting and makes testing easier.\\n- Performance: Zustand uses subscriptions to individual parts of the state, which prevents redundant re-renders of components unrelated to the data that has changed.\\n- Flexibility: it integrates with TypeScript easily, supports middleware (for logging or saving to localStorage, for example) and is not tied to React (it can be used in plain JavaScript).\\n\\nThe main disadvantages\\n\\n- A smaller ecosystem: compared with Redux, Zustand has fewer ready-made solutions, extensions and learning materials.\\n- Limited support for complex scenarios: to manage complex side effects (chains of asynchronous requests, for example) additional logic or middleware may be required, whereas Redux Toolkit offers built-in means (createAsyncThunk).\\n- A risk when scaling: in very large applications with dozens of stores confusion can arise if a clear architecture is not observed. Zustand does not impose strict rules, which can lead to chaos.\\n- Debugging: the developer tools (DevTools) for Zustand are less developed than the Redux DevTools, although basic logging is available.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nZustand is an excellent choice for projects where simplicity, the speed of development and performance without superfluous abstractions matter. It is worth applying in small and medium applications, in prototypes, or when Redux seems excessive. For large projects with strict requirements on the architecture and the extensibility, it is better to consider Redux Toolkit or MobX.","import { create } from 'zustand';\\n\\n// We create the store\\nconst useStore = create((set) => ({\\n  count: 0,\\n  increment: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count + 1 })),\\n  decrement: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count - 1 })),\\n}));\\n\\n// A component uses the store\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const count = useStore((state) => state.count);\\n  const increment = useStore((state) => state.increment);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <span>{count}</span>\\n      <button onClick={increment}>+</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2425,"SWR (stale-while-revalidate) is a library for React, which makes obtaining data from a server easier. It caches the responses automatically, shows the stale data on repeat requests and updates it in the background. That improves the performance and the UX, making the application faster.","What is SWR?\\nSWR (stale-while-revalidate) is a library for React created by Vercel, which implements an HTTP caching strategy. It lets you show the user the stale data from the cache first and then perform a request to the server in the background in order to obtain fresh data (revalidate). That makes the interface responsive and fast, especially on a slow connection.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nSWR uses React Hooks. You pass in a key (normally a URL) and a fetcher function (fetch, for example). The library manages the state of the data, the errors and the loading automatically. It also supports revalidation when the window gains focus, interval-based checking and optimistic updates.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nSWR is ideal for applications where the speed of the response and the currency of the data matter: dashboards, social networks, online shops. It reduces the number of requests to the server and improves the user experience.\\n\\nConclusion: SWR is worth applying in React applications where efficient management of data from a server is required, especially with frequent updates or slow networks.","import useSWR from 'swr';\\n\\nconst fetcher = (url) => fetch(url).then(res => res.json());\\n\\nfunction Profile() {\\n  const { data, error, isLoading } = useSWR('/api/user', fetcher);\\n\\n  if (error) return <div>Loading error</div>;\\n  if (isLoading) return <div>Loading...</div>;\\n  return <div>Hello, {data.name}!</div>;\\n}"],[2426,"In React, Error Boundaries are used for handling errors — components that catch errors in the child tree and display a fallback UI. An Error Boundary is created by means of the componentDidCatch or getDerivedStateFromError methods. Error Boundaries do not catch errors in asynchronous code, in event handlers or in server rendering, however — those need their own try/catch.","What is an Error Boundary?\\nAn Error Boundary is a React component, which intercepts JavaScript errors anywhere in the child component tree, logs them and displays a fallback interface instead of the component that has crashed. It is the analogue of try/catch for the React tree.\\n\\nHow do you create an Error Boundary?\\nAn Error Boundary is created by means of the lifecycle methods \`static getDerivedStateFromError()\` and \`componentDidCatch()\`. The first updates the state in order to display the fallback UI, the second logs the error.\\n\\nWhere do Error Boundaries not work?\\n\\n- In event handlers (you have to use try/catch inside them)\\n- In asynchronous code (setTimeout, fetch)\\n- During server rendering\\n- In the Error Boundary itself (an error in it is not caught)\\n\\nThe modern approach\\nSince React 16 Error Boundaries have been the standard way. For functional components there is no direct analogue, but you can use libraries such as \`react-error-boundary\`.\\n\\nConclusion: Error Boundaries are obligatory for production applications, in order to prevent the UI crashing completely and to improve the user experience. For asynchronous operations and events, use try/catch or wrapper libraries.","class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {\\n  constructor(props) {\\n    super(props);\\n    this.state = { hasError: false };\\n  }\\n\\n  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {\\n    return { hasError: true };\\n  }\\n\\n  componentDidCatch(error, errorInfo) {\\n    console.error('Error caught:', error, errorInfo);\\n  }\\n\\n  render() {\\n    if (this.state.hasError) {\\n      return <h1>Something went wrong.</h1>;\\n    }\\n    return this.props.children;\\n  }\\n}"],[2427,"An Error Boundary cannot intercept errors in event handlers, in asynchronous code (setTimeout, requestAnimationFrame), during server rendering, or its own errors inside the Error Boundary itself. It also does not catch errors thrown outside React's rendering cycle.","What an Error Boundary is, and its limitations\\nAn Error Boundary is a React component, which catches JavaScript errors in the child component tree during the rendering, in the lifecycle methods and in the constructors. It is not a universal interceptor of every error in an application, however.\\n\\nWhich errors are not intercepted\\n\\n- Event handlers: errors inside onClick, onChange and other events do not reach the Error Boundary, since they run outside React's rendering cycle. To handle them, use an ordinary try/catch.\\n- Asynchronous code: errors in setTimeout, setInterval or a Promise (including async/await) are not intercepted. An example:\\n\\nYou have to use try/catch, or .catch() for promises.\\n- Server rendering (SSR): an Error Boundary works only in the browser; on the server it is not applied.\\n- The Error Boundary's own errors: if the boundary component itself throws an error, it will not be intercepted by that same component.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nTo handle such cases, use the global window.onerror handler or try/catch.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn Error Boundary is useful for isolating rendering errors, but it does not replace the other methods of handling errors. For events and asynchronous code, apply try/catch or special libraries.","setTimeout(() => { throw new Error('An error'); }, 1000);\\n\\nclass ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {\\n  state = { hasError: false };\\n  static getDerivedStateFromError() { return { hasError: true }; }\\n  render() {\\n    if (this.state.hasError) return <h1>An error</h1>;\\n    return this.props.children;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The error in the handler will not be caught\\n<button onClick={() => { throw new Error('Click error'); }}>Click</button>"],[2428,"SWR uses the stale-while-revalidate strategy: it returns the cached (stale) data first, then performs a request to the server and updates the cache. That provides an instant response from the interface together with current data. The cache is held in memory and can be global or local. SWR manages repeat requests automatically when the window gains focus or the network is restored.","How the caching works in SWR\\nSWR (stale-while-revalidate) is a library for fetching data in React, which implements an HTTP caching strategy. The main idea is to show the user the stale data from the cache first and then to perform a request to the server in the background in order to obtain fresh data. That lets the interface be responsive rather than waiting for the network request to complete.\\n\\nThe mechanism of its working\\n\\n- On the first call of \`useSWR\` a request to the server is performed and the result is saved in the cache.\\n- On a repeat call with the same key, SWR first returns the data from the cache (stale), then initiates a new request (revalidate).\\n- Once the response has arrived, the cache is updated and the component repaints with the new data.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nManaging the cache\\nSWR uses a global cache by default, but you can configure your own cache provider. The request's key (the first argument of \`useSWR\`) serves as the identifier for the caching. If the key changes, the old cache for the previous key stays, but the new request is performed afresh.\\n\\nConclusion\\nCaching in SWR is ideal for applications where the speed of the response and the currency of the data matter — in dashboards or social networks, for example. It reduces the delays and lowers the load on the server, updating the data automatically when that is needed.","import useSWR from 'swr';\\n\\nconst fetcher = (url) => fetch(url).then(res => res.json());\\n\\nfunction Profile() {\\n  const { data, error } = useSWR('/api/user', fetcher);\\n\\n  if (error) return <div>Loading error</div>;\\n  if (!data) return <div>Loading...</div>;\\n  return <div>Hello, {data.name}!</div>;\\n}"],[2429,"Zustand is a minimalist library for managing state with a simple hook-based API, which requires no boilerplate. Redux Toolkit is a more structured solution with the concepts of slices, reducers and middleware, which suits large applications. Zustand is simpler to learn and faster to set up, whereas Redux Toolkit offers strict patterns and powerful tools for complex scenarios.","The main differences between Zustand and Redux Toolkit\\nZustand and Redux Toolkit are two popular libraries for managing state in React applications, but they have fundamentally different approaches. Zustand offers a minimalist API based on hooks, without the need for Provider wrappers or complex configuration. Redux Toolkit, on the contrary, is an evolution of the classic Redux and provides a structured set of tools, including createSlice, createAsyncThunk and built-in support for middleware.\\n\\nThe architecture and the API\\nIn Zustand the state and the actions are defined in a single object, and access to them is obtained through the useStore hook. For example:\\n\\nIn Redux Toolkit you have to create a slice and a reducer and connect the store through a Provider:\\n\\nPerformance and size\\nZustand is smaller (about 1 KB) and requires no additional dependencies, which makes it ideal for small and medium projects. Redux Toolkit is bigger (about 11 KB) and includes tools such as Immer for immutability and the Redux DevTools for debugging. Zustand supports DevTools too, but through a separate configuration.\\n\\nThe scenarios of use\\nZustand suits simple applications better, where fast, lightweight state management without superfluous boilerplate is needed. Redux Toolkit is recommended for large projects with a great many pieces of state, complex logic and a need for strict typing (especially with TypeScript).\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice between Zustand and Redux Toolkit depends on the scale of the project: for small and medium applications Zustand provides simplicity and speed, while for large, complex systems Redux Toolkit offers structure and powerful tools.","import { create } from 'zustand';\\n\\nconst useStore = create((set) => ({\\n  count: 0,\\n  increment: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count + 1 })),\\n}));\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const { count, increment } = useStore();\\n  return <button onClick={increment}>{count}</button>;\\n}\\n\\nimport { createSlice, configureStore } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\n\\nconst counterSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'counter',\\n  initialState: { count: 0 },\\n  reducers: {\\n    increment: (state) => { state.count += 1; },\\n  },\\n});\\n\\nconst store = configureStore({ reducer: counterSlice.reducer });\\n\\n// In the component:\\nimport { useDispatch, useSelector } from 'react-redux';\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const count = useSelector((state) => state.count);\\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\\n  return <button onClick={() => dispatch(increment())}>{count}</button>;\\n}"],[2430,"The onboarding begins with a familiarization with the project's documentation, the setting up of the environment and a study of the architecture. A new developer is assigned a mentor, who helps with the first tasks. Code review and the gradual increase in the complexity of the tasks play an important role. The aim is to bring the developer into the context quickly and to make them productive.","What onboarding is, and what it is for\\nOnboarding is the process of introducing a new developer to the project, the team and the working processes. In large frontend projects it is critically important, since the codebase can contain thousands of files, a complex architecture and a great many dependencies. Without structured onboarding a new employee can spend weeks studying things on their own, which reduces the team's productivity.\\n\\nThe main stages of the onboarding\\n\\n- Preparing the environment: setting the repository up, installing the dependencies, running the project locally. A script or a Docker container is often used for the automation.\\n- The documentation: the README, the wiki, the architectural diagrams, the code-style guides. The documentation should be current and should contain the answers to the frequent questions.\\n- Mentorship: the assignment of an experienced developer, who helps with the first tasks, explains the processes and answers the questions.\\n- The first tasks: normally small bugs or simple features, so that the new developer can become familiar with the code and the tools.\\n- Code review: regular reviews help a newcomer learn the best practices and adapt to the team's standards faster.\\n\\nAn example of a script for setting the environment up\\n\\nConclusion\\nStructured onboarding speeds the adaptation up, reduces the number of mistakes and improves developers' satisfaction. It is especially useful in large projects with high complexity and a great many participants.","#!/bin/bash\\n# setup.sh — the automatic set-up of the project\\ngit clone git@github.com:company/frontend.git\\ncd frontend\\nnpm install\\ncp .env.example .env\\ndocker-compose up -d\\necho \\"The environment is ready!\\""],[2431,"Micro-frontends are an architectural approach in which a frontend application is broken into independent, loosely coupled modules. Every module is developed, tested and deployed separately, which lets the teams work in parallel. That improves the scalability and makes maintaining large projects easier.","What are micro-frontends?\\nMicro-frontends are an architectural style in which a web application's interface is broken into small, independent parts, each of which is responsible for its own functionality. This approach borrows ideas from the microservice architecture on the backend but applies them to the frontend. The main aim is to avoid a monolithic frontend, which is hard to maintain and to scale.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nEvery micro-frontend is a separate application, which can be written in a different framework (React, Vue, Angular) and managed by its own team. The integration happens at the level of the browser, by means of techniques such as iframes, Web Components or special frameworks (Module Federation in Webpack 5, for example).\\n\\nAn example of the integration through Module Federation\\n\\nIn the main application that component can be loaded dynamically:\\n\\nThe advantages and the disadvantages\\n\\n- The advantages: independent development, isolated deployment, technological flexibility, improved scalability.\\n- The disadvantages: the complexity of the integration, an increase in the size of the bundle, the need for a single design system, potential performance problems.\\n\\nConclusion: micro-frontends are worth applying in large projects with several teams, where the independence of the development and the deployment matters. For small applications this approach can be excessive, because of the additional complexity.","// webpack.config.js for the micro-frontend\\nconst ModuleFederationPlugin = require('webpack/lib/container/ModuleFederationPlugin');\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  plugins: [\\n    new ModuleFederationPlugin({\\n      name: 'app1',\\n      filename: 'remoteEntry.js',\\n      exposes: {\\n        './Header': './src/Header',\\n      },\\n      shared: ['react', 'react-dom'],\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n};\\n\\n// The main application\\nconst Header = React.lazy(() => import('app1/Header'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <React.Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n      <Header />\\n    </React.Suspense>\\n  );\\n}"],[2432,"The statuses of asynchronous tasks are normally held as a finite state machine with the states: idle, loading, success, error. That lets you track the progress and handle the errors. In React useState or useReducer is used for that, and in Redux separate fields in the slices. Such an approach makes debugging easier and improves the user experience.","The basic concept\\nAsynchronous tasks such as requests to an API require the states to be managed, so that the interface responds properly to the loading, the success or the error. Rather than a simple boolean flag (isLoading, for example), it is recommended that you use a finite state machine with four states: idle (waiting), loading, success and error. That lets you determine the current status exactly and avoid logical errors.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation in React\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nSuch an approach is used in any application with asynchronous operations: from simple forms to complex dashboards. In Redux separate slices with status, data and error fields are often created. That makes testing and maintaining the code easier.\\n\\nConclusion\\nHolding the statuses of asynchronous tasks as a finite state machine makes the code predictable and resistant to errors. It is recommended that you apply this pattern in every project that has asynchronous requests, in order to improve the user experience and to make debugging easier.","const [status, setStatus] = useState('idle');\\nconst [data, setData] = useState(null);\\nconst [error, setError] = useState(null);\\n\\nconst fetchData = async () => {\\n  setStatus('loading');\\n  try {\\n    const response = await fetch('/api/data');\\n    const result = await response.json();\\n    setData(result);\\n    setStatus('success');\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    setError(err.message);\\n    setStatus('error');\\n  }\\n};"],[2433,"The onChange handler in React is called on every change of an input field's value. It lets you update the component's state, making the field controlled. That ensures the synchronization between the state and the interface and gives you the opportunity to validate or transform the input.","The purpose of the onChange handler\\nA handler for a change of an input field's value (normally \`onChange\` in React) is used in order to track every input of the user in real time. It is necessary for creating controlled components, where React's state is the single source of truth for the field's value.\\n\\nHow this works\\nWhen the user types text, the browser generates an \`input\` event. React wraps it in a synthetic \`onChange\` event. You can obtain the current value through \`event.target.value\` and update the component's state by means of \`setState\` or the \`useState\` hook.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Forms: registration, login, search.\\n- Filters and search boxes with autocompletion.\\n- Validating the input in real time (checking an email, for example).\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe onChange handler is obligatory for controlled components in React. It provides control over the input and makes validation and the integration with the other parts of the application easier. Use it whenever you need to respond to changes of the text in input fields.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction MyInput() {\\n  const [value, setValue] = useState('');\\n\\n  const handleChange = (event) => {\\n    setValue(event.target.value);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <input type=\\"text\\" value={value} onChange={handleChange} />\\n  );\\n}"],[2434,"It makes sense to split the state into independent parts when different parts of the data change independently of one another. That improves the readability of the code, makes testing easier and prevents unnecessary repaints of the components. The state of a form and the state of the data loading, for example, are better held separately.","Why split the state?\\nIn React applications the state can quickly become complex if everything is held in one object. Splitting it into independent parts (by means of several \`useState\` calls or \`useReducer\`, for example) lets each part change without affecting the others. That reduces the risk of accidental side effects and makes debugging easier.\\n\\nWhen is this especially useful?\\n\\n- When the data has a different frequency of updating (text input and an animation, for example).\\n- When the parts of the state relate to different logical entities (the user and a list of tasks, for example).\\n- When redundant repaints have to be avoided — React repaints a component only when the part of the state it uses changes.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nSplitting the state into independent parts is a basic practice for maintaining the cleanliness and the performance of React components. Apply it whenever the parts of the data are not logically related or are updated at a different rate.","// Bad: everything in one object\\nconst [state, setState] = useState({ user: null, posts: [], loading: false });\\n\\n// Good: split into independent parts\\nconst [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\nconst [posts, setPosts] = useState([]);\\nconst [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);"],[2435,"No, secret data cannot be held in a frontend application's environment variables. Every environment variable used in the client code ends up in the final bundle and becomes available to any user through the browser's developer tools. To hold secrets (API keys, tokens) you have to use the application's server side.","Why this is unsafe\\nEnvironment variables in frontend applications — in React or Vue, for example — are substituted at the build stage. Webpack or another bundler replaces every occurrence of \`process.env.API_KEY\` with the actual value. As a result that data ends up in the JavaScript files that are downloaded into the user's browser.\\n\\nHow this works in practice\\nLet us look at an example with Create React App:\\n\\nAfter the build, the production bundle will simply contain the string 'secret123'. Any user can open the DevTools, find that string in the sources and use it.\\n\\nThe right approach\\nSecret data should be held only on the server. The frontend can address its own backend, which already uses the secrets in order to work with external APIs. For example:\\n\\nConclusion\\nNever hold secrets in the frontend's environment variables. Use a server proxy or a BFF (Backend For Frontend) for working with confidential data safely. That is a basic principle of web-application security.","// The .env file\\nREACT_APP_API_KEY=secret123\\n\\n// In the component's code\\nconst apiKey = process.env.REACT_APP_API_KEY;\\nconsole.log(apiKey); // 'secret123'\\n\\n// The frontend (React)\\nconst response = await fetch('/api/data');\\n\\n// The backend (Node.js)\\napp.get('/api/data', (req, res) => {\\n  const secret = process.env.API_SECRET; // safe\\n  const data = await fetchExternalAPI(secret);\\n  res.json(data);\\n});"],[2436,"To change a list element's status in React, you have to use the map method, which creates a new array. Inside the map you find the element by id and return a copy of it with the updated status. Then the new array is set through setState. It matters not to mutate the original array but to create a new one.","Changing a list element's status in React\\nIn React the state has to be updated immutably — that is, without changing the existing data directly. For lists that means we cannot simply change a property of an object inside the array; a new array with the updated element has to be created.\\n\\nThe main approach\\nUse the \`map\` method, which goes through every element of the array and returns a new array. Inside the callback, find the element you need (by id, for example) and return a copy of it with the status changed. For the remaining elements, return them unchanged.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nImmutability guarantees that React can track the changes efficiently and repaint only the components that really have changed. A direct mutation (\`items[0].status = 'new'\`, for example) will not cause a re-render, since the reference to the array will stay the same.\\n\\nThe alternative methods\\n\\n- findIndex + splice (not recommended) — it creates a copy of the array through a spread, then changes the element by index.\\n- filter + concat — for removing or adding elements, but not for updating them.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse \`map\` in order to update a list element's status in React. That is a simple, readable way, in accordance with the principles of immutability, which guarantees the virtual DOM works properly and prevents bugs with the state.","const [items, setItems] = useState([\\n  { id: 1, status: 'active' },\\n  { id: 2, status: 'inactive' }\\n]);\\n\\nconst toggleStatus = (id) => {\\n  setItems(prevItems =>\\n    prevItems.map(item =>\\n      item.id === id\\n        ? { ...item, status: item.status === 'active' ? 'inactive' : 'active' }\\n        : item\\n    )\\n  );\\n};"],[2437,"To toggle a task's status in React, you have to hold the array of tasks in the component's state. On a click on a task a function is called, which finds the task by id and changes its status to the opposite. Then the state is updated by means of setState or the useState hook.","Managing the state of tasks in React\\nToggling a task's status is a typical task when working with lists in React. The main idea is to hold an array of task objects in the component's state and, on every action of the user (a click on a checkbox, for example), to update that array, changing the status property of the particular task.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\nLet us look at a simple TodoList component, which displays a list of tasks and lets you toggle their status from 'done' to 'not done' and back.\\n\\nThe key points\\n\\n- The functional form of setState (prevTasks => ...) is used, in order to guarantee the previous state is current.\\n- The map method creates a new array without mutating the original, which accords with the principles of immutability in React.\\n- The spread operator (...task) copies all of the task's properties, and then only completed is overridden.\\n\\nConclusion\\nSuch an approach is applied in any React application where lists of data have to be managed with the ability to change individual elements. It is simple, efficient and easy to scale to more complex scenarios — using Redux or the Context API, for example.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TodoList() {\\n  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState([\\n    { id: 1, text: 'Learn React', completed: false },\\n    { id: 2, text: 'Write some code', completed: true }\\n  ]);\\n\\n  const toggleTask = (taskId) => {\\n    setTasks(prevTasks =>\\n      prevTasks.map(task =>\\n        task.id === taskId\\n          ? { ...task, completed: !task.completed }\\n          : task\\n      )\\n    );\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {tasks.map(task => (\\n        <li key={task.id}>\\n          <input\\n            type=\\"checkbox\\"\\n            checked={task.completed}\\n            onChange={() => toggleTask(task.id)}\\n          />\\n          {task.text}\\n        </li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}"],[2438,"Code is worth extracting into separate components when it is repeated in several places, performs one particular task or becomes too complex to read. That improves the reuse, the testing and the maintenance of the code. The main principle is that every component should be responsible for one logical unit of the interface.","When to extract code into separate components\\nExtracting code into separate components is a key practice in React, based on the single responsibility principle. A component should do one thing and do it well. If you notice that part of the JSX or of the logic is repeated, that it makes the parent component harder to read, or that it could be reused elsewhere, the time has come for a refactor.\\n\\nThe main criteria\\n\\n- Repetition: one and the same block of code occurs in different parts of the application. A button with a particular style and behaviour, for example.\\n- Complexity: the component contains a lot of nested elements, conditional logic or event handlers. Splitting it into smaller parts makes it easier to understand.\\n- Reuse: the logic or the interface may be needed in other projects or modules. A custom input or a modal window, for example.\\n- Testing: small components are easier to test in isolation than one big monolithic block.\\n\\nAn example\\nSuppose you have a list of users, where every item displays an avatar, a name and an email. Rather than writing all of the markup inside the parent component, we extract the item into a separate \`UserCard\` component.\\n\\nNow \`UserCard\` can be reused anywhere a user has to be displayed. The parent component has become cleaner and is responsible only for rendering the list.\\n\\nConclusion\\nExtract code into separate components when that improves the readability or the reuse or makes testing easier. Do not do it prematurely — begin with a simple structure and refactor as the application grows.","// UserCard.jsx\\nfunction UserCard({ user }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\"user-card\\">\\n      <img src={user.avatar} alt={user.name} />\\n      <h3>{user.name}</h3>\\n      <p>{user.email}</p>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// UserList.jsx\\nfunction UserList({ users }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      {users.map(user => (\\n        <UserCard key={user.id} user={user} />\\n      ))}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2439,"In React, to clear an input field after an element has been added, you have to reset the corresponding piece of state to an empty string. If a controlled component is used, the field's value is bound to the state through value and onChange. Once the addition has been handled (on a press of a button, for example), it is enough to call setState with an empty string. That guarantees the interface is synchronized with the data.","Clearing an input field in React\\nIn React controlled components are the standard approach to working with forms. An input field's value is held in the component's state and is updated through the onChange handler. When the user adds an element (to a list of tasks, for example), you have to not only handle the new data but also reset the input field, for convenience in further use.\\n\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at a simple component for adding elements to a list:\\n\\nIn this example inputValue manages the field's content. After the element is added, setInputValue('') is called, which clears the input field immediately. It matters that the clearing happens after a successful addition rather than before it, in order to avoid losing the data.\\n\\nThe alternative approaches\\nSometimes developers use uncontrolled components with a ref, in order to access the DOM element directly. That is less preferable, however, since it breaks the principle of a single source of truth in React. In most cases controlled components are simpler to debug and to test.\\n\\nConclusion: clearing an input field after an element has been added is standard practice for improving the user experience. In React it is implemented through resetting the controlled component's state. It is recommended that you use this approach in any form where data has to be added — in lists of tasks, in comments or in feedback forms, for example.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TodoList() {\\n  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);\\n  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState('');\\n\\n  const handleAdd = () => {\\n    if (inputValue.trim() === '') return;\\n    setItems([...items, inputValue]);\\n    setInputValue(''); // We clear the field\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input\\n        type=\\"text\\"\\n        value={inputValue}\\n        onChange={(e) => setInputValue(e.target.value)}\\n      />\\n      <button onClick={handleAdd}>Add</button>\\n      <ul>\\n        {items.map((item, index) => (\\n          <li key={index}>{item}</li>\\n        ))}\\n      </ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2440,"Environment variables in a React application are used at the build stage (build time). They are embedded in the code when the build is started through Create React App or analogous tools. That lets you set different configurations for different environments (development, production) without changing the source code.","What are environment variables in React?\\nEnvironment variables are parameters that are passed to an application from outside, normally through the operating system or configuration files. In React applications they are used for holding sensitive data (API keys, for example) or settings that change depending on the environment (development, testing, production).\\n\\nAt what stage are they applied?\\nIn React applications created by means of Create React App (CRA) or similar tools, the environment variables are processed at the build stage (build time). That means they are embedded in the static JavaScript files while the \`npm run build\` command runs. After the build they cannot be changed without a repeat build.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nDuring the build CRA replaces every occurrence of \`process.env.REACT_APP_*\` with the corresponding value from the \`.env\` file or from the system's environment. For example:\\n\\nImportant: only variables with the \`REACT_APP_\` prefix will be available in the code. That is done for safety, so that the system's variables are not revealed by accident.\\n\\nA practical example\\nSuppose you have an application that has to work with different APIs in development and in production. You create the files \`.env.development\` and \`.env.production\`:\\n\\nWhen \`npm start\` is run (development mode) the first file is used; with \`npm run build\` the second. In the code you simply address \`process.env.REACT_APP_API_URL\`.\\n\\nConclusion\\nEnvironment variables in React are applied at the build stage, in order to configure the application for different environments. That is convenient for managing API keys, URLs and other settings, but it requires a repeat build when they change. Use them for separating the configurations between development and production.","// The .env file\\nREACT_APP_API_URL=https://api.example.com\\n\\n// In the React code\\nconst apiUrl = process.env.REACT_APP_API_URL;\\nconsole.log(apiUrl); // 'https://api.example.com' after the build\\n\\n// .env.development\\nREACT_APP_API_URL=http://localhost:3000/api\\n\\n// .env.production\\nREACT_APP_API_URL=https://myapp.com/api"],[2441,"A React application obtains the API server's address through environment variables. In projects on Create React App, variables with the REACT_APP_ prefix are used — REACT_APP_API_URL, for example. They are set in .env files or passed in at start-up. In Next.js the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix is used. That lets you change the API's address without changing the code.","The ways of configuring the API URL in React\\nA React application has no direct access to the server's file system, so the API server's address is normally passed in through environment variables. That is the standard approach to separating the configuration of development, testing and production.\\n\\nEnvironment variables in Create React App\\nIn projects created by means of Create React App (CRA), every environment variable that has to be available in the browser must begin with the \`REACT_APP_\` prefix. That is done for safety, so that the server's secrets are not revealed by accident.\\n\\nAn example of the usage:\\n\\nFor the different environments you can create the files \`.env.development\`, \`.env.production\` and \`.env.local\`. CRA loads the file needed automatically, depending on the value of \`NODE_ENV\`.\\n\\nEnvironment variables in Next.js\\nIn Next.js the \`NEXT_PUBLIC_\` prefix is used for the variables available on the client:\\n\\nThe alternative approaches\\n\\n- A configuration file: you can create a \`config.js\` file, which exports an object with the settings, and import it in the places where it is needed. This approach does not let you change the configuration for different environments easily without a rebuild, however.\\n- A proxy server: in development mode you can configure a proxy in \`package.json\` (the \`proxy\` field), in order to redirect the requests to another server. That is convenient for local development, but it does not solve the problem of the configuration for production.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUsing environment variables with the corresponding prefixes is the standard, safe way of configuring the API URL in React applications. It lets you switch between the environments easily without changing the code and accords with the best practices of development.","// The .env file at the root of the project\\nREACT_APP_API_URL=https://api.example.com\\n\\n// In a React component's code\\nconst apiUrl = process.env.REACT_APP_API_URL;\\n\\nfetch(\`\${apiUrl}/users\`)\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data));\\n\\n// .env.local\\nNEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.example.com\\n\\n// In the component\\nconst apiUrl = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL;"],[2442,"Environment variables in React are the values that are held outside the application's code and used in order to configure its behaviour in different environments (development, production). In React projects created with Create React App they are set in a .env file and are available through process.env.REACT_APP_*. That lets you hold API keys, server URLs and other settings safely, without including them in the source code.","What are environment variables in React?\\nEnvironment variables are a way of passing configuration data to an application that can change depending on the runtime environment: development, testing or production. In React they are especially useful for holding API URLs, access keys for third-party services and other parameters that should not be hard-wired into the code.\\n\\nHow do they work in React?\\nIn projects created by means of Create React App (CRA), the environment variables are set in \`.env\` files at the root of the project. For a variable to be available in the code, its name has to begin with the \`REACT_APP_\` prefix. For example:\\n\\nAfter that, in the React code you can address them through \`process.env.REACT_APP_API_URL\`. CRA substitutes those values automatically at the build stage, replacing them with the real strings.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nIn development mode the variables are loaded from the \`.env.development\` file, and in production from \`.env.production\`. That lets you switch between the environments easily.\\n\\nWhere is this applied?\\n\\n- Holding the backend's URL for the different environments.\\n- Managing the keys for analytics or maps.\\n- Switching features on and off through flags (\`REACT_APP_FEATURE_NEW_UI=true\`, for example).\\n\\nConclusion\\nEnvironment variables are the standard way of configuring React applications, which improves the safety and the flexibility. Use them in order to separate the settings from the code, especially when working with different deployment environments.","REACT_APP_API_URL=https://api.example.com\\nREACT_APP_API_KEY=your-secret-key\\n\\nimport React from 'react';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const apiUrl = process.env.REACT_APP_API_URL;\\n  return <div>API URL: {apiUrl}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nexport default App;"],[2443,"CRUD in React is implemented through the management of state (useState) and the performing of HTTP requests (fetch/axios). The components display the data, the forms for creating and editing it, and the buttons for deleting it. The state is updated after a successful response from the server, which ensures the UI stays synchronized with the data.","The basics of CRUD in React\\nCRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) are the basic operations on data, which are implemented in React through a combination of local state and asynchronous requests to an API. The \`useState\` hook is normally used for holding the list of items, and \`useEffect\` for the initial loading of the data.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\nLet us look at a simple application for managing tasks. The component uses state for holding the list of tasks, and functions for each operation:\\n\\nThe key points\\n\\n- Use \`useEffect\` to load the data when the component mounts.\\n- For every operation (POST, PUT, DELETE), send the corresponding HTTP request.\\n- Update the state only after the response from the server has arrived, in order to avoid falling out of sync.\\n- For complex applications, consider using Redux or React Query for state management and caching.\\n\\nConclusion: CRUD in React is a standard pattern, which is easy to implement by means of hooks and fetch. It suits any application in which an interaction with a REST API is required, from simple lists of tasks to complex control panels.","import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TodoApp() {\\n  const [todos, setTodos] = useState([]);\\n  const [newTodo, setNewTodo] = useState('');\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch('/api/todos')\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => setTodos(data));\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const addTodo = () => {\\n    fetch('/api/todos', {\\n      method: 'POST',\\n      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n      body: JSON.stringify({ title: newTodo })\\n    })\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(todo => setTodos([...todos, todo]));\\n  };\\n\\n  const updateTodo = (id, title) => {\\n    fetch(\`/api/todos/\${id}\`, {\\n      method: 'PUT',\\n      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n      body: JSON.stringify({ title })\\n    })\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(updated => setTodos(todos.map(t => t.id === id ? updated : t)));\\n  };\\n\\n  const deleteTodo = (id) => {\\n    fetch(\`/api/todos/\${id}\`, { method: 'DELETE' })\\n      .then(() => setTodos(todos.filter(t => t.id !== id)));\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input value={newTodo} onChange={e => setNewTodo(e.target.value)} />\\n      <button onClick={addTodo}>Add</button>\\n      <ul>\\n        {todos.map(todo => (\\n          <li key={todo.id}>\\n            {todo.title}\\n            <button onClick={() => updateTodo(todo.id, 'Updated')}>Edit</button>\\n            <button onClick={() => deleteTodo(todo.id)}>Delete</button>\\n          </li>\\n        ))}\\n      </ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2444,"In React applications Redux, MobX, Zustand, Recoil and the built-in Context API are used for managing state. Redux is popular for large projects with predictable state. MobX uses reactive programming. Zustand is a lightweight alternative. The Context API suits the simple cases, without additional libraries.","An overview of the state-management libraries in React\\nState management is a key task in React applications, especially when the data is passed between a great many components. The built-in \`useState\` and \`useReducer\` suit local state, but global or complex state requires specialized solutions.\\n\\nThe main libraries\\n\\n- Redux — predictable state through a single store and pure reducers. It uses a unidirectional data flow. It suits large applications with a great many interactions.\\n- MobX — reactive management, in which the state consists of observable objects. It updates the components automatically when they change. It is convenient for applications with frequent updates of the data.\\n- Zustand — a minimalist library with a simple API. It creates the store through a hook and requires no wrappers. It is ideal for medium projects.\\n- Recoil — an experimental library from Facebook; it uses atoms and selectors. It integrates well with React's Concurrent Mode.\\n- The Context API — the built-in tool for passing data through the component tree. It suits the simple cases, but it can cause redundant re-renders.\\n\\nAn example of the code with Zustand\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of library depends on the scale of the project and the team's preferences. Redux remains the standard for large applications, Zustand for simplicity, and the Context API for minimal dependencies. It matters to understand the trade-offs between performance and complexity.","import { create } from 'zustand';\\n\\nconst useStore = create((set) => ({\\n  count: 0,\\n  increment: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count + 1 })),\\n  decrement: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count - 1 })),\\n}));\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const { count, increment, decrement } = useStore();\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={decrement}>-</button>\\n      <span>{count}</span>\\n      <button onClick={increment}>+</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2445,"MobX is a library for managing state, based on reactive programming. Its main advantage is simplicity and minimalist code: the state tracks the dependencies automatically and updates only what is needed. The disadvantages: the difficulty of debugging because of the implicit updates, less popularity than Redux, and possible performance problems with a large number of observable objects.","What MobX is, and what it is for\\nMobX is a library for managing state, which makes the data reactive. It tracks the changes and updates the user interface automatically, minimizing the developer's manual work. Unlike Redux, where the actions and the reducers have to be described explicitly, MobX lets you work with the state as with ordinary JavaScript objects, adding the magic of reactivity to them.\\n\\nMobX's advantages\\n\\n- Simplicity and minimalism: to begin working, it is enough to wrap the state in \`observable\` and the components in \`observer\`. The code becomes shorter and more comprehensible.\\n- Automatic optimization: MobX tracks exactly which parts of the state are used in each component and repaints only those. That reduces the number of redundant renders.\\n- Flexibility: you can use classes or a functional approach, and combine it with other libraries easily.\\n\\nMobX's disadvantages\\n\\n- The difficulty of debugging: because of the implicit updates it can be hard to understand why the state has changed. The developer tools are less developed than Redux's.\\n- Less popularity: the community and the number of ready-made solutions are smaller than Redux's, which can make it harder to find the answers to specific questions.\\n- Performance problems: with a large number of observable objects or deep nesting, delays can arise if the memoization is not configured by hand.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example \`CounterStore\` makes the \`count\` property reactive automatically. The \`Counter\` component is wrapped in \`observer\`, so it will repaint only when \`count\` changes.\\n\\nConclusion\\nMobX is worth applying in small and medium projects, where the speed of development and the simplicity of the code matter. It is ideal for prototypes and applications with uncomplicated state logic. For large projects with many developers and strict requirements on predictability, it is better to choose Redux or other, more formalized solutions.","import { makeAutoObservable } from 'mobx';\\nimport { observer } from 'mobx-react-lite';\\n\\nclass CounterStore {\\n  count = 0;\\n\\n  constructor() {\\n    makeAutoObservable(this);\\n  }\\n\\n  increment() {\\n    this.count++;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst store = new CounterStore();\\n\\nconst Counter = observer(() => {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {store.count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => store.increment()}>+</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n});"],[2446,"A side effect is any change of state or interaction with the outside world inside a function that is not related directly to its return value. Examples: requests to an API, working with localStorage, changing the DOM. In React side effects are handled by means of the useEffect hook, so as not to break the purity of the rendering.","What is a side effect?\\nA side effect is any action of a function that changes state outside its own scope or interacts with the outside world. In functional programming functions should be pure: they take arguments and return a result without changing anything outside. Side effects break that purity, so they have to be controlled.\\n\\nExamples of side effects\\n\\n- Requests to a server (fetch, axios)\\n- Changing the DOM directly\\n- Working with localStorage or cookies\\n- Setting timers (setTimeout, setInterval)\\n- Logging to the console\\n\\nSide effects in React\\nIn React components should be pure functions of the props and the state. Side effects are moved into the useEffect hook, which runs after the rendering. That guarantees the rendering stays predictable.\\n\\nConclusion\\nSide effects are inevitable in real applications, but they have to be isolated from the pure logic. In React, use useEffect for working with external resources, in order to preserve the predictability of the components and to make testing easier.","import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // The side effect: a request to an API\\n    fetch(\`/api/users/\${userId}\`)\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => setUser(data));\\n  }, [userId]); // The dependency: the effect restarts when userId changes\\n\\n  return <div>{user ? user.name : 'Loading...'}</div>;\\n}"],[2447,"useContext is a React hook, which lets you read a context's value and subscribe to its changes. It is used for passing data through the component tree without an explicit passing of props at every level. Simply call useContext(MyContext) inside a component and it will return the context's current value.","What is useContext?\\nuseContext is a React hook, which provides access to the value of a context created by means of the Context API. It solves the problem of prop drilling, where data has to be passed through a great many nested components. Rather than passing props by hand, you can create a context and use it in any descendant component.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nFirst a context is created by means of React.createContext(). Then a Provider wraps the component tree and passes the value in. Any component inside the provider can call useContext(MyContext) and obtain that value. When the context's value changes, all of the subscribed components re-render.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen to use it?\\nuseContext is ideal for global data such as the theme, the localization or the authentication. You should not abuse it for frequent updates of state, however, since that can cause redundant renders. In such cases it is better to use Redux or other state-management libraries.\\n\\nConclusion: useContext is a simple, efficient way of sharing data in a React application, especially for static or rarely changing values.","import React, { createContext, useContext, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nconst ThemeContext = createContext('light');\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('dark');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={theme}>\\n      <Toolbar />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Toolbar() {\\n  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  return <div>Current theme: {theme}</div>;\\n}"],[2448,"Side effects are any changes of a program's state or interactions with the outside world that happen while a function runs. They include: changing global variables, writing to a file, sending an HTTP request, printing to the console, changing the DOM. Pure functions should have no side effects, which makes them predictable and testable.","What are side effects?\\nSide effects are any operations performed by a function that affect the program's state beyond its own local scope or interact with the outside world. In functional programming a pure function should have no side effects: it only takes arguments and returns a result, without changing anything outside itself.\\n\\nExamples of side effects\\n\\n- Changing a global variable or an object's property\\n- Writing to a file or a database\\n- Sending an HTTP request\\n- Printing to the console (console.log)\\n- Changing the DOM\\n- Generating random numbers\\n- Obtaining the current time\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nAn understanding of side effects matters when working with React (useEffect) and Redux (reducers have to be pure), and in functional programming in JavaScript, Python and other languages. Isolating the side effects makes the code more predictable and makes testing easier.\\n\\nConclusion: side effects are inevitable in real applications, but they should be minimized and moved to the boundaries of the system, so that the main logic stays pure and testable.","// A function with a side effect (changing a global variable)\\nlet counter = 0;\\nfunction increment() {\\n  counter++; // side effect\\n  return counter;\\n}\\n\\n// A pure function without side effects\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n  return a + b;\\n}\\n\\n// A function with a side effect (writing to the console)\\nfunction greet(name) {\\n  console.log('Hello, ' + name); // side effect\\n  return 'Hello, ' + name;\\n}"],[2449,"To count the re-renders, use useRef, which preserves a value between renders without causing a new render. Create a counter through useRef(0) and increment it in the component's body. Then print the value in a useEffect or directly in the JSX. That lets you track how many times the component has updated.","Counting the re-renders in React\\nWhen a component updates, React runs its function again. To find out how many times that has happened, you cannot use useState, since changing it would cause a new render, creating an infinite loop. useRef is applied instead — it holds a mutable value, which does not cause a re-render when it changes.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe counter increments on every iteration of the render. A useEffect with an empty array of dependencies will not do, since it fires only after mounting. Use a useEffect without dependencies, in order to log after every render.\\n\\nWhere this is useful\\n\\n- Optimizing the performance — revealing redundant re-renders.\\n- Debugging — understanding which props or state cause the updates.\\n- Learning — a visual demonstration of how React works.\\n\\nConclusion: use useRef in order to count the re-renders when you need to track the frequency of the updates without affecting the rendering process itself.","import { useRef, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  const renderCount = useRef(0);\\n  renderCount.current += 1;\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log('Render number:', renderCount.current);\\n  });\\n\\n  return <div>Renders: {renderCount.current}</div>;\\n}"],[2450,"With asynchronous state updates in React, problems with stale data can arise, where a closure captures the old value. Race conditions are also possible if several asynchronous operations update the state at the same time. To solve that, you have to use the functional form of setState or useReducer.","The problems of asynchronous state updates\\nIn React the updating of state is asynchronous, which can lead to unexpected bugs, especially when working with closures or parallel requests. The main problems: a stale closure and race conditions.\\n\\nA stale closure\\nWhen you use the current state inside an asynchronous callback (in a setTimeout or a useEffect, for example), the closure can capture a stale value if the state has changed after the callback was created. An example:\\n\\nThe solution: use the functional form setCount(prev => prev + 1), which always receives the current value.\\n\\nRace conditions\\nWith several asynchronous requests updating one piece of state, the result can depend on the order in which the requests complete. In a search with autocompletion, for example:\\n\\nThe solution: use the cancellation of the previous requests (AbortController) or check the currency of the request through a flag.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo avoid problems with asynchronous state updates, use the functional form of setState, cancel stale requests and apply useReducer for complex logic. That matters especially in components with frequent updates, such as forms or a search.","const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const timer = setTimeout(() => {\\n    setCount(count + 1); // count may be stale\\n  }, 1000);\\n}, []);\\n\\nconst [results, setResults] = useState([]);\\nconst fetchData = async (query) => {\\n  const data = await fetch('/api?q=' + query);\\n  setResults(data); // it may overwrite newer data\\n};"],[2451,"To optimize a React component with a timer, use the useEffect hook to set the timer up and be sure to clean it up in the cleanup function, in order to avoid memory leaks. Use useRef for holding the timer's identifier, so that it is preserved between renders. That guarantees the timer will be stopped properly when the component unmounts or the dependencies change.","Optimizing a React component with a timer\\nWhen working with timers in React, it matters to manage their lifecycle properly, in order to avoid memory leaks and incorrect behaviour. The main problem arises when the component unmounts while the timer keeps running, causing an update of the state of a component that no longer exists.\\n\\nUsing useEffect and useRef\\nThe \`useEffect\` hook lets you perform side effects such as setting timers up. Inside the effect you can create a timer by means of \`setInterval\` or \`setTimeout\`. The key point is to return a clean-up function, which will be called when the component unmounts or before the effect runs again. The \`useRef\` hook is used for holding the timer's identifier, so that it is not lost on every render.\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Always clean your timers up in the cleanup function, in order to avoid leaks.\\n- Use \`useRef\` for holding the timer's identifier if it is needed outside the effect (for stopping it by hand, for example).\\n- State the right dependencies in \`useEffect\`'s array of dependencies, in order to avoid the timer being recreated needlessly.\\n\\nConclusion\\nOptimizing a React component with a timer comes down to the proper management of the side effects through \`useEffect\` and \`useRef\`. That prevents memory leaks and guarantees the application works stably, especially with frequent mounting and unmounting of the components.","import React, { useState, useEffect, useRef } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TimerComponent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const intervalRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    intervalRef.current = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    return () => {\\n      clearInterval(intervalRef.current);\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies — the effect runs once\\n\\n  return <div>Count: {count}</div>;\\n}"],[2452,"The problem arises because of the closure, which captures the state's value at the moment the setInterval callback was created. To avoid it, you can use the functional form of setState (prev => prev + 1), use useRef for holding the current value, or recreate the interval when a dependency changes, through useEffect.","The problem of stale values in setInterval\\nWhen you use setInterval inside a useEffect or an event handler, the interval's callback is created once and captures the state's value at the moment of its creation. That is the classic problem of closures in JavaScript. If you write setInterval(() => setCount(count + 1), 1000), for example, then count inside the callback will always equal the value it had on the first render rather than the updated one.\\n\\nThe solution with a functional update\\nThe simplest way is to use the functional form of setState, which receives the previous state as its argument:\\n\\nThat works because React guarantees prev will be the current value at the moment the update is performed.\\n\\nThe solution with useRef\\nIf you need to use the state's current value inside the callback (for logging or for conditions, for example), use useRef for holding the current value:\\n\\nuseRef does not cause a repaint and always contains the current value, since you update it on every render.\\n\\nThe solution with recreating the interval\\nYou can add count to useEffect's dependencies, so that the interval is recreated on every change of the state:\\n\\nThis approach is less efficient, since the interval will constantly be created and cleared, but it is intuitively comprehensible.\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor a simple increment, use a functional update of setState. If you need to read the current state inside the callback, apply useRef. Recreating the interval through the dependencies is the fallback, when the other methods do not fit.","useEffect(() => {\\n  const id = setInterval(() => {\\n    setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n  }, 1000);\\n  return () => clearInterval(id);\\n}, []);\\n\\nconst countRef = useRef(count);\\ncountRef.current = count;\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const id = setInterval(() => {\\n    console.log(countRef.current);\\n  }, 1000);\\n  return () => clearInterval(id);\\n}, []);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const id = setInterval(() => {\\n    setCount(count + 1);\\n  }, 1000);\\n  return () => clearInterval(id);\\n}, [count]);"],[2453,"React can make the performance worse with a large number of elements because of the reconciliation mechanism. On every update of the state React compares the virtual DOM with the real one, which for thousands of elements requires a great deal of computation. The absence of keys, or their wrong use, forces React to repaint every element rather than only the ones that have changed.","Why React can drag on large lists\\nReact uses a virtual DOM in order to optimize the updates, but with a large number of elements (10,000 rows in a table, for example) the reconciliation process becomes costly. Every time a component's state changes, React creates a new virtual DOM tree and compares it with the previous one. For thousands of elements that leads to noticeable delays.\\n\\nThe role of keys\\nKeys help React identify the elements when a list is updated. If the keys are not set or the array indexes are used, React may repaint every element even if only one has changed. The right keys (unique and stable) let React minimize the changes.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nAdditional optimizations\\n\\n- Virtualization (react-window, react-virtualized) — rendering only the visible elements.\\n- Memoization (React.memo, useMemo) — preventing redundant repaints.\\n- Avoiding frequent updates of the state.\\n\\nConclusion: for large lists, use keys, memoization and virtualization, in order to avoid a collapse in the performance.","// Bad: without keys, or with the index\\nfunction List({ items }) {\\n  return items.map((item, index) => <div key={index}>{item.name}</div>);\\n}\\n\\n// Good: with a unique id\\nfunction List({ items }) {\\n  return items.map(item => <div key={item.id}>{item.name}</div>);\\n}"],[2454,"React does not solve the performance problem with a large number of elements without virtualization. When rendering thousands of elements, React creates and updates a great many DOM nodes, which leads to things slowing down. For such cases you have to use virtualization libraries — react-window or react-virtualized, for example.","React's performance problem with large lists\\nReact in itself does not optimize the rendering of a large number of elements. When you render a list of 10,000 items, React creates 10,000 DOM nodes, which causes considerable delays in the loading and the updating of the page. That happens because the browser spends a lot of time processing and repainting such a number of nodes.\\n\\nHow virtualization works\\nVirtualization solves that problem by rendering only the elements that are visible in the viewport. The remaining elements are replaced with empty placeholders or are not rendered at all. That lets you work with lists of hundreds of thousands of elements without a loss of performance.\\n\\nAn example without virtualization (the problematic one)\\n\\nAn example with virtualization (the solution)\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact provides no built-in means of working with large lists. To ensure high performance when rendering thousands of elements, you must use virtualization libraries such as react-window or react-virtualized.","function LargeList() {\\n  const items = Array.from({ length: 10000 }, (_, i) => i);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      {items.map(item => (\\n        <div key={item}>Item {item}</div>\\n      ))}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nimport { FixedSizeList } from 'react-window';\\n\\nfunction VirtualizedList() {\\n  const items = Array.from({ length: 10000 }, (_, i) => i);\\n  const Row = ({ index, style }) => (\\n    <div style={style}>Item {items[index]}</div>\\n  );\\n  return (\\n    <FixedSizeList height={400} itemCount={items.length} itemSize={35}>\\n      {Row}\\n    </FixedSizeList>\\n  );\\n}"],[2455,"Virtualized lists display only the visible elements, so a search over all of the data requires its own mechanism. The search works only over the data that has been loaded rather than over the whole set. For a full search you have to hold the complete array of data and filter it before passing it into the virtualized list.","The limitations of searching in virtualized lists\\nVirtualized lists (React Virtualized or react-window, for example) display only a small number of the elements, the ones visible in the viewport. That creates limitations for a search, since the data that is not displayed is physically absent from the DOM.\\n\\nThe main problems\\n\\n- A search only over the visible data: if the search is performed over the current DOM, it will find only the elements that have been rendered. The remaining data stays unavailable.\\n- The need for a separate store: for a proper search you have to hold the complete array of data outside the virtualized list and filter it before passing it in.\\n- A reset of the scrolling: after the filtering the list often resets its scroll position, which can disorient the user.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\n\\nIn this example the search works over the complete array, while the virtualization is applied only to the filtered data.\\n\\nConclusion\\nVirtualized lists require their own management of the data for a search. Hold the complete set of data outside the list and filter it before the rendering, in order to avoid the limitations of the virtualization.","const allItems = Array.from({ length: 10000 }, (_, i) => ({ id: i, text: \`Item \${i}\` }));\\nconst [filter, setFilter] = useState('');\\nconst filteredItems = allItems.filter(item => item.text.includes(filter));\\n\\nreturn (\\n  <div>\\n    <input value={filter} onChange={e => setFilter(e.target.value)} />\\n    <FixedSizeList height={400} itemCount={filteredItems.length} itemSize={35} width={300}>\\n      {({ index, style }) => (\\n        <div style={style}>{filteredItems[index].text}</div>\\n      )}\\n    </FixedSizeList>\\n  </div>\\n);"],[2456,"Memoization becomes unprofitable when the cost of comparing the props (a shallow compare) and of holding the cached values exceeds the cost of simply re-rendering the component. That often happens with simple components that render fast, or when the props change almost every time. In such cases the memoization only adds overhead without any real benefit.","When memoization harms the performance\\nMemoization in React (through React.memo, useMemo, useCallback) is a technique for caching the results of computations or of components. It is not always useful, however. The main rule: memoization is justified if the cost of a repeat render or computation is higher than the cost of comparing the dependencies and holding the cache.\\n\\nThe main scenarios in which memoization is superfluous\\n\\n- Simple components: if a component renders only one div with some text, re-rendering it costs pennies. Adding a React.memo with a shallow compare of the props can be more expensive than the render itself.\\n- Props that change often: if the props (objects or functions, for example) are created anew on every render of the parent, the memoization will constantly reset the cache, adding redundant work.\\n- Stable trees: if the child elements do not change (static content, for example), the memoization gives no gain but does consume memory.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nHow to assess whether memoization is needed\\nUse the React DevTools Profiler in order to measure the render time. If a component renders for longer than 1–2 ms and its props change rarely, memoization may help. If the render takes less than 0.1 ms, the memoization is most likely superfluous.\\n\\nConclusion: memoization is a tool for optimization rather than a panacea. Apply it only to heavy components or computations, where profiling shows real bottlenecks. In every other case, prefer simple code without memoization.","// Bad: the memoization of a simple component\\nconst SimpleButton = React.memo(({ label }) => {\\n  return <button>{label}</button>;\\n});\\n\\n// Better: without memoization\\nconst SimpleButton = ({ label }) => {\\n  return <button>{label}</button>;\\n};\\n\\n// An example with useMemo for heavy computations\\nconst expensiveResult = useMemo(() => {\\n  return heavyCalculation(data);\\n}, [data]); // Useful if heavyCalculation is expensive"],[2457,"In React the child component mounts earlier than the parent, so its useEffect runs first. React renders the child component first, then starts its effects, and only after that moves on to the parent. That is because React processes the component tree from the bottom up.","The order in which useEffect runs in React\\nIn React the order in which the effects (useEffect) run depends on how the components mount into the DOM. When you have a parent and a child component, React renders the child component first, since it is part of the parent. Once the rendering of the child component has completed, React starts its effects, and only then moves on to the parent component.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhy does this happen?\\nReact uses the reconciliation algorithm, which processes the component tree depth-first. The child component is rendered first, then its effects, and only then the parent. That guarantees the child elements are completely ready before the parent component performs its own side effects.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of the order in which useEffect runs matters for managing the dependencies and preventing errors, especially when working with asynchronous operations or subscriptions. In most cases this behaviour is intuitive, but it is worth taking into account when designing complex components.","import React, { useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Child() {\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log('Child useEffect');\\n  }, []);\\n  return <div>Child</div>;\\n}\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log('Parent useEffect');\\n  }, []);\\n  return <Child />;\\n}\\n\\n// The output in the console:\\n// Child useEffect\\n// Parent useEffect"],[2458,"The History API lets you change the URL in the browser's address bar without the page reloading. The pushState() and replaceState() methods add or replace entries in the browser's history. That is used in SPAs for navigating between virtual pages. The popstate event fires on a press of the \\"Back\\" or \\"Forward\\" buttons.","What the History API is, and what it is for in an SPA\\nIn single-page applications (SPAs) all of the logic works on one HTML page. So that the user can move between the sections and still see the URL change (/about, /products, for example), the History API is used. It lets you change the address bar without a full reload of the page, which makes the navigation fast and smooth.\\n\\nThe History API's main methods\\n\\n- pushState(state, title, url) — it adds a new entry to the browser's history and changes the URL. The first argument is a state object, which can be passed back when you return to that entry.\\n- replaceState(state, title, url) — it replaces the current entry in the history without creating a new one. It is useful for updating the URL without adding superfluous steps.\\n- The popstate event — it fires when the user presses the \\"Back\\" or \\"Forward\\" buttons. In the handler you can obtain the saved state and update the interface.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nThe History API is used in the modern frameworks (React Router, Vue Router) for implementing client-side routing. It is also useful when creating interactive widgets, where state has to be preserved in the URL (filters on a products page, for example).\\n\\nConclusion: the History API is the key tool for building an SPA with convenient navigation. It is worth applying when the URL has to change without the page reloading, while the browser's \\"Back\\" and \\"Forward\\" buttons remain usable.","// Moving to the /about page without a reload\\nhistory.pushState({ page: 'about' }, '', '/about');\\n\\n// Handling a press of the \\"Back\\" button\\nwindow.addEventListener('popstate', (event) => {\\n  if (event.state) {\\n    console.log('We have returned to the page:', event.state.page);\\n    // We update the interface in accordance with the state\\n  }\\n});"],[2459,"An SPA (Single Page Application) is a web application that loads a single HTML page and updates its content dynamically without a reload. All of the routing and rendering logic is performed on the client by means of JavaScript. SPAs' popularity is due to the fast response of the interface, the smooth navigation and the reduced load on the server.","What is an SPA?\\nAn SPA (Single Page Application) is a web application that works in the browser and does not require a full reload of the page when moving between the sections. All of the markup, styles and scripts needed are loaded once, on the first request. The further interaction with the user happens through asynchronous requests to the server (normally through AJAX or the Fetch API) and the dynamic updating of the DOM.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nThe basis of an SPA is a client-side router, which intercepts clicks on links and changes the URL without the page reloading. The corresponding component or view is then displayed. The data is normally obtained through a REST API or GraphQL. An example of a simple router in JavaScript:\\n\\nWhy has the SPA become popular?\\n\\n- A fast response: after the initial load the interface responds instantly, since there is no need to wait for a response from the server for every page.\\n- Smooth navigation: the transitions between the sections look like animations, without flicker or a reload.\\n- A reduced load on the server: the server delivers only the data (JSON) rather than finished HTML, which reduces the traffic and the processing time.\\n- Convenience in development: a clear separation of the frontend and the backend, and the ability to use the modern frameworks (React, Vue, Angular).\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn SPA is ideal for applications with intensive user interaction: social networks, control panels, online editors. For content sites (blogs, news), however, it is better to use SSR or static generators, because of the problems with SEO and with the initial load.","const routes = {\\n  '/': () => renderHome(),\\n  '/about': () => renderAbout(),\\n  '/contact': () => renderContact()\\n};\\n\\nfunction navigate(path) {\\n  history.pushState(null, null, path);\\n  routes[path]();\\n}\\n\\nwindow.addEventListener('popstate', () => {\\n  routes[window.location.pathname]();\\n});"],[2460,"React uses a comparison (diffing) algorithm, based on two assumptions: elements of different types create different trees, and keys help it match the elements in lists. On an update React compares the virtual DOM trees, starting from the root. If an element's type has changed, React recreates the whole subtree. If the type matches, React updates the attributes and processes the child elements recursively. The keys let it update lists efficiently, minimizing the repainting.","How does React determine which nodes have to be updated?\\nReact uses a comparison algorithm (reconciliation), which works with the virtual DOM. Rather than rebuilding the tree completely on every change, React applies a heuristic approach with two key rules:\\n- Elements of different types give rise to different trees. If the root element's type has changed (a \`<div>\` has become a \`<span>\`, for example), React deletes the old subtree completely and creates a new one.\\n- The developer can state keys (\`key\`) for the child elements, so that React can match them between renders. That matters especially for lists.\\n\\nThe comparison algorithm\\nReact compares two virtual DOM trees, starting from the root node. If the nodes' types match, React updates only the attributes (the props) and processes the child elements recursively. If the types differ, React deletes the old node and all of its descendants, then creates a new node from scratch. For the child elements React uses the keys for optimization: if the keys match, React moves or updates the existing element rather than creating a new one.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact's comparison algorithm lets it update the user interface efficiently, minimizing the number of operations on the real DOM. An understanding of this mechanism helps you write performant components, especially when working with dynamic lists and frequent updates.","// An example with keys\\nfunction List({ items }) {\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {items.map(item => (\\n        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// When the order of the elements changes, React uses the keys\\n// in order to move the existing DOM nodes rather than recreating them"],[2461,"Optimistic updates in RTK Query let you update the interface at once, before the response from the server arrives, which makes the application more responsive. The \`onQueryStarted\` option in a mutation is used for that, where you can apply \`patchQueryResult\` in order to change the cache temporarily. If the request ends in an error, the changes are rolled back by means of \`undo\`. That is especially useful for operations that almost always succeed — likes or adding to favourites, for example.","What are optimistic updates?\\nOptimistic updates are the technique in which the interface is updated immediately after the user's action, without waiting for the response from the server. That creates a feeling of an instant reaction and improves the UX. In the event of an error the changes are rolled back and the user sees the current state.\\n\\nThe implementation in RTK Query\\nRTK Query provides built-in support for optimistic updates through the \`onQueryStarted\` callback in the definition of a mutation. Inside that callback you can use \`patchQueryResult\` in order to change the data in the cache temporarily and then roll it back on an error.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe key points\\n\\n- \`updateQueryData\` — the function for changing the cache; it returns an object with an \`undo\` method.\\n- It matters to handle the errors, in order to roll the changes back and possibly show a notification.\\n- It suits operations where the probability of an error is low — likes, editing text.\\n\\nConclusion\\nOptimistic updates in RTK Query are a powerful tool for creating responsive interfaces. They are worth applying in scenarios where the user expects instant feedback and server errors are rare or can be handled gracefully.","import { createApi, fetchBaseQuery } from '@reduxjs/toolkit/query/react';\\n\\nconst api = createApi({\\n  baseQuery: fetchBaseQuery({ baseUrl: '/api' }),\\n  endpoints: (builder) => ({\\n    updatePost: builder.mutation({\\n      query: ({ id, ...patch }) => ({\\n        url: \`posts/\${id}\`,\\n        method: 'PATCH',\\n        body: patch,\\n      }),\\n      onQueryStarted: async ({ id, ...patch }, { dispatch, queryFulfilled }) => {\\n        // The optimistic update\\n        const patchResult = dispatch(\\n          api.util.updateQueryData('getPost', id, (draft) => {\\n            Object.assign(draft, patch);\\n          })\\n        );\\n        try {\\n          await queryFulfilled;\\n        } catch {\\n          // The roll-back on an error\\n          patchResult.undo();\\n        }\\n      },\\n    }),\\n  }),\\n});"],[2462,"Stale-While-Revalidate is a caching strategy in which the stale data is shown to the user at once while a request for an update is performed in the background. In React Query it is implemented through the staleTime and cacheTime parameters. staleTime determines how long the data counts as fresh, after which it is marked as stale. On a repeat request React Query returns the cached data first and then performs a background refetch automatically.","What is Stale-While-Revalidate?\\nStale-While-Revalidate (SWR) is a caching strategy, which lets you balance between the speed of the response and the currency of the data. The main idea is that when data is requested, the cached (possibly stale) version is returned first, and then a request to the server for fresh data is performed in the background. That provides an instant response for the user together with the gradual updating of the information.\\n\\nThe implementation in React Query\\nReact Query (now TanStack Query) uses this strategy by default. The key configuration parameters:\\n- staleTime — the time in milliseconds during which the data counts as fresh. By default 0 — that is, the data counts as stale at once.\\n- cacheTime — the time the data is held in the cache after it has stopped being used. By default 5 minutes.\\n\\nWhen a component requests data, React Query checks the cache:\\n- If the data is there and is not stale (staleTime has not expired), it is returned at once.\\n- If the data is there but is stale, the cached data is returned and a background refetch is started.\\n- If there is no data, an ordinary request is performed.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nStale-While-Revalidate in React Query lets you create fast, responsive interfaces, minimizing the delays for the user. This strategy is especially useful for applications where the speed of the response matters and the data can be slightly stale (a news feed or a list of products, for example).","import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';\\n\\nfunction UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  const { data, isLoading, isStale } = useQuery({\\n    queryKey: ['user', userId],\\n    queryFn: () => fetch(\`/api/users/\${userId}\`).then(res => res.json()),\\n    staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000, // the data counts as fresh for 5 minutes\\n    cacheTime: 10 * 60 * 1000, // it is held in the cache for 10 minutes\\n  });\\n\\n  if (isLoading) return <div>Loading...</div>;\\n  return <div>{data.name} {isStale ? '(updating...)' : ''}</div>;\\n}"],[2463,"In an SPA the content changes without a reload thanks to client-side routing and the dynamic loading of data. When a link is followed, JavaScript intercepts the event, updates the URL through the History API and replaces part of the DOM. The data is obtained asynchronously through AJAX or the Fetch API, which lets you update only the blocks of the interface that are needed.","How an SPA updates the content without a reload\\nIn single-page applications (SPAs) all of the navigation and display logic is performed on the client side. Rather than sending a request to the server for new HTML, JavaScript takes over the management of the routes and of the interface's state. That is achieved through a combination of client-side routing, the History API and asynchronous requests.\\n\\nClient-side routing and the History API\\nOn a click on a link or a button, the browser's event is intercepted and the standard transition is cancelled. Instead the \`history.pushState\` method is called, which changes the URL in the address bar without the page reloading. The router then analyses the new path and renders the corresponding component or view.\\n\\nThe asynchronous loading of data\\nTo obtain the new content, an SPA uses AJAX or the Fetch API. That lets it request only the data needed (JSON, for example) and update part of the DOM without touching the rest of the page. Such an approach reduces the volume of the data transferred and speeds the interaction up.\\n\\nThe virtual DOM and reactivity\\nThe modern frameworks (React, Vue, Angular) additionally use a virtual DOM. They compare the new and the old state of the interface and apply only the minimal changes to the real DOM. That makes the updates even more efficient and smooth.\\n\\nConclusion: an SPA changes the content without a reload thanks to client-side routing, the History API and asynchronous requests. Such an approach is justified for applications with intensive user interaction, where the speed of the response and the smoothness of the transitions matter.","// An example of a simple router in JavaScript\\nconst routes = {\\n  '/': () => renderHome(),\\n  '/about': () => renderAbout(),\\n};\\n\\nfunction navigate(path) {\\n  history.pushState(null, '', path);\\n  routes[path]();\\n}\\n\\n// Intercepting the clicks on the links\\ndocument.querySelectorAll('a').forEach(link => {\\n  link.addEventListener('click', (e) => {\\n    e.preventDefault();\\n    navigate(link.getAttribute('href'));\\n  });\\n});\\n\\n// An example of loading the data for a profile page\\nasync function loadProfile(userId) {\\n  const response = await fetch(\`/api/users/\${userId}\`);\\n  const user = await response.json();\\n  document.getElementById('profile').innerHTML = \`\\n    <h2>\${user.name}</h2>\\n    <p>\${user.email}</p>\\n  \`;\\n}"],[2464,"The main alternatives to Redux: the Context API for the simple cases, MobX with its reactive approach, Zustand with minimal boilerplate, Recoil from Facebook, Jotai with its atomic state. The choice depends on the application's complexity and the team's preferences.","The alternatives to Redux for managing state\\nRedux was the standard for managing state in React for a long time, but its redundancy and complexity led to the appearance of a great many alternatives. Each of them solves the problem of state management in its own way, offering a different balance between simplicity, performance and scalability.\\n\\nThe main alternatives\\n\\n- The Context API — the tool built into React for passing data through the component tree. It suits simple applications with a small amount of global state. An example of the usage:\\n\\n- MobX — it uses reactive programming, in which the state consists of observable objects and the components repaint automatically when they change. An example:\\n\\n- Zustand — a minimalist library with a simple hook-based API. It requires no wrappers or providers. An example:\\n\\n- Recoil — a library from Facebook with atomic state, in which every atom is an independent unit. It suits complex dependencies between pieces of data.\\n- Jotai — similar to Recoil, but with an even simpler API and a smaller size.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of an alternative to Redux depends on the size of the project and the performance requirements. For small applications the Context API is enough; for medium ones Zustand or Jotai; for large ones with reactive data MobX or Recoil. The main thing is not to complicate the architecture unnecessarily.","const ThemeContext = React.createContext('light');\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value=\\"dark\\">\\n      <Toolbar />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nimport { observable, action } from 'mobx';\\nclass Store {\\n  @observable count = 0;\\n  @action increment() { this.count++; }\\n}\\n\\nimport create from 'zustand';\\nconst useStore = create((set) => ({\\n  count: 0,\\n  increment: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count + 1 })),\\n}));"],[2465,"To create a reusable table with different columns, you have to pass the configuration of the columns in through props. Every column contains a header and a function for rendering the cell. The table component takes an array of data and an array of columns and then renders the rows and the cells on the basis of that configuration. That lets you change the set of columns easily without changing the component itself.","The concept of a reusable table component\\nIn React tabular data often has to be displayed, but the set of columns can change from screen to screen. Rather than creating a separate table for every case, you can create one universal component, which takes the configuration of the columns plus the data. That reduces the duplication of code and makes maintenance easier.\\n\\nHow this works\\nThe Table component receives two main props: \`data\` (an array of objects) and \`columns\` (an array of objects describing each column). Every column object contains at least a \`header\` and a \`render\` (a function that returns the JSX for the cell). The render function receives the current item of data and can display any content.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\n\\nAdditional capabilities\\n\\n- Sorting: you can add an \`onSort\` prop and sorting state.\\n- Filtering: pass a filtering function or filter components into the column's configuration.\\n- Customizing the styles: through a \`className\` prop, or styles for individual columns.\\n\\nConclusion\\nSuch an approach lets you adapt a table to different tasks easily without rewriting the logic. It is especially useful in large projects, where there are many screens with tables, and it helps you observe the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle.","const Table = ({ data, columns }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <table>\\n      <thead>\\n        <tr>\\n          {columns.map(col => (\\n            <th key={col.key}>{col.header}</th>\\n          ))}\\n        </tr>\\n      </thead>\\n      <tbody>\\n        {data.map((row, index) => (\\n          <tr key={index}>\\n            {columns.map(col => (\\n              <td key={col.key}>{col.render(row)}</td>\\n            ))}\\n          </tr>\\n        ))}\\n      </tbody>\\n    </table>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// The usage\\nconst columns = [\\n  { key: 'name', header: 'Name', render: (row) => row.name },\\n  { key: 'age', header: 'Age', render: (row) => row.age },\\n  { key: 'action', header: 'Action', render: (row) => <button>Delete</button> },\\n];\\n\\n<Table data={users} columns={columns} />"],[2466,"Client-side routing in an SPA handles the transitions between pages on the browser side without the page reloading, using JavaScript and the History API. Server-side navigation sends a request to the server, which returns new HTML, causing a full reload. Client-side routing is faster and creates a smooth user experience, but it requires the whole application to be loaded initially. Server-side navigation is simpler for SEO and works without JavaScript.","The main difference\\nClient-side routing in an SPA (Single Page Application) handles the navigation on the browser side without a full reload of the page. Server-side navigation, on the contrary, sends a request to the server on every transition, and the server returns a new HTML document, causing a full reload.\\n\\nHow client-side routing works\\nIn an SPA the application is loaded once, and then JavaScript intercepts the clicks on the links, changes the URL by means of the History API (pushState or replaceState) and updates the page's content dynamically. That lets it avoid the delays related to loading the resources afresh.\\n\\nHow server-side navigation works\\nWith server-side navigation every transition is a new HTTP request. The server handles the URL, generates the HTML and sends it to the client. The browser reloads the page completely, resetting the application's state.\\n\\nWhen to use which\\nClient-side routing suits interactive applications where the speed of the response and the smoothness of the transitions matter (dashboards, social networks, for example). Server-side navigation is better for content sites, where SEO optimization and a fast first render matter (blogs, news portals, for example).\\n\\nConclusion: the choice between client-side and server-side routing depends on the requirements on the performance, the SEO and the application's complexity. For an SPA with high interactivity, client-side routing is preferable; for content sites, server-side.","// An example with React Router\\nimport { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route, Link } from 'react-router-dom';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <BrowserRouter>\\n      <nav>\\n        <Link to=\\"/\\">Home</Link>\\n        <Link to=\\"/about\\">About</Link>\\n      </nav>\\n      <Routes>\\n        <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Home />} />\\n        <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n      </Routes>\\n    </BrowserRouter>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// An example of a server route in Express\\napp.get('/about', (req, res) => {\\n  res.send('<h1>About Page</h1><p>Server-rendered content</p>');\\n});"],[2467,"The separation is achieved by extracting a presentational component, responsible only for drawing the table, and a container, which manages the data and the logic. The presentational component receives the data through props and calls callbacks for the actions. The container prepares the data, handles the events and passes them into the presentational component. That makes testing and reuse easier.","Why separate the components\\nWhen working with tables, there is often a temptation to put all of the logic — loading the data, sorting, filtering, pagination — directly into the component that renders the rows and the cells. That leads to strong coupling and makes testing, reuse and maintenance harder. The separation into a presentational and a container component solves that problem.\\n\\nThe presentational component\\nIt is responsible only for displaying the table. It does not know where the data comes from or how it is processed. It takes the data through props, plus callbacks for the actions (sorting or a click on a row, for example).\\n\\nThe container component\\nIt manages the state and the business logic: it loads the data and handles the sorting, the filtering and the pagination. It then passes the prepared data and the callbacks into the presentational component.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe separation into a presentational and a container component makes the code more modular, more testable and more reusable. The presentational component can easily be replaced or used in another context, while the container can be tested in isolation. This approach is especially useful in large projects with a great many tables and complex logic for working with the data.","// Table.jsx\\nfunction Table({ data, columns, onSort, onRowClick }) {\\n  return (\\n    <table>\\n      <thead>\\n        <tr>\\n          {columns.map(col => (\\n            <th key={col.key} onClick={() => onSort(col.key)}>\\n              {col.label}\\n            </th>\\n          ))}\\n        </tr>\\n      </thead>\\n      <tbody>\\n        {data.map(row => (\\n          <tr key={row.id} onClick={() => onRowClick(row)}>\\n            {columns.map(col => (\\n              <td key={col.key}>{row[col.key]}</td>\\n            ))}\\n          </tr>\\n        ))}\\n      </tbody>\\n    </table>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// TableContainer.jsx\\nfunction TableContainer() {\\n  const [data, setData] = useState([]);\\n  const [sortKey, setSortKey] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch('/api/data')\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(setData);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const handleSort = (key) => {\\n    const sorted = [...data].sort((a, b) => a[key] > b[key] ? 1 : -1);\\n    setData(sorted);\\n    setSortKey(key);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <Table\\n      data={data}\\n      columns={[\\n        { key: 'name', label: 'Name' },\\n        { key: 'age', label: 'Age' }\\n      ]}\\n      onSort={handleSort}\\n      onRowClick={(row) => console.log(row)}\\n    />\\n  );\\n}"],[2468,"A table's data can be held inside the component (local state) or outside it (global state, Redux, the server). Local state is simpler, but it does not scale. External storage lets you share the data between components and makes testing easier. The choice depends on the application's complexity and on whether synchronization is needed.","Where to hold a table's data: locally or globally?\\nA table's data can sit inside the component (in its local state) or be moved out into an external store such as Redux, React context, or server state through libraries like React Query. That choice directly affects the application's architecture, its scalability and its maintainability.\\n\\nLocal storage\\nWhen the data is held inside the component, it is isolated and available only to that component. That is convenient for simple, independent tables that do not have to be synchronized with other parts of the application.\\n\\nThe pros: simplicity, the absence of dependencies. The cons: the data cannot easily be passed to another component, and isolation is hard to test.\\n\\nExternal storage\\nWhen the data is held outside — in a Redux store, for example — it becomes globally available. That is necessary for tables that are displayed on several pages or are updated from different sources.\\n\\nThe pros: centralized management, ease of synchronization, easier testing through mocks. The cons: redundancy for the simple cases, a more complicated architecture.\\n\\nThe effect on the architecture\\n\\n- Local state suits small, isolated components (a dropdown list, for example).\\n- Global state is justified for data that is used in different parts of the application (a table of orders in an admin panel, for example).\\n- Server state (through React Query) is the best choice for data obtained from an API, since it manages the caching and the updating automatically.\\n\\nConclusion: hold the data locally if the table is independent and requires no synchronization. Use an external store when the data has to be available to several components or requires complex update logic. That will keep the architecture flexible and maintainable.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction SimpleTable() {\\n  const [data, setData] = useState([\\n    { id: 1, name: 'Alice' },\\n    { id: 2, name: 'Bob' }\\n  ]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <table>\\n      {data.map(row => (\\n        <tr key={row.id}><td>{row.name}</td></tr>\\n      ))}\\n    </table>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// actions.js\\nexport const setTableData = (data) => ({\\n  type: 'SET_TABLE_DATA',\\n  payload: data\\n});\\n\\n// reducer.js\\nconst initialState = { tableData: [] };\\nexport default function tableReducer(state = initialState, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'SET_TABLE_DATA':\\n      return { ...state, tableData: action.payload };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}"],[2469,"To avoid duplicating a table's business logic, move the common logic (the pagination, the sorting, the filtering) into a custom hook — useTable, for example. The table component takes props and uses that hook. In the different places in the application you simply call the hook with different settings and pass the result into one and the same table component. That lets you reuse both the logic and the presentation without copying code.","The problem of duplicating a table's business logic\\nWhen one and the same table component is used in different parts of an application, there is often a temptation to copy the pagination, sorting and filtering logic into every parent component. That leads to duplicated code, makes maintenance harder and increases the risk of bugs.\\n\\nThe solution: a custom hook plus a reusable component\\nThe best approach is to move the common business logic into a separate custom hook — \`useTable\`, for example. That hook manages the state of the page, the sorting and the filtering and provides the methods for changing them. The table component itself becomes presentational — it takes the data and the callbacks through props and contains no logic.\\n\\nThe application in the different parts of the application\\nNow in any component where a table is needed, you simply use the hook and pass the result into the component:\\n\\nSimilarly for an orders page — you simply call \`useTable(ordersData, { pageSize: 10 })\` and pass different columns in.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUsing a custom hook for a table's business logic and a presentational component for the display lets you avoid duplicated code entirely. This approach scales easily, makes testing easier and makes the application more maintainable. It is recommended for any repeated UI pattern with shared logic.","// useTable.js\\nimport { useState, useMemo } from 'react';\\n\\nexport function useTable(data, config) {\\n  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);\\n  const [sortBy, setSortBy] = useState(null);\\n  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('');\\n\\n  const processedData = useMemo(() => {\\n    let result = [...data];\\n    if (filter) {\\n      result = result.filter(item =>\\n        item.name.toLowerCase().includes(filter.toLowerCase())\\n      );\\n    }\\n    if (sortBy) {\\n      result.sort((a, b) => (a[sortBy] > b[sortBy] ? 1 : -1));\\n    }\\n    const pageSize = config.pageSize || 10;\\n    const start = (page - 1) * pageSize;\\n    return result.slice(start, start + pageSize);\\n  }, [data, page, sortBy, filter, config]);\\n\\n  return { processedData, page, setPage, sortBy, setSortBy, filter, setFilter };\\n}\\n\\n// Table.jsx\\nfunction Table({ data, onSort, onFilter, columns }) {\\n  return (\\n    <table>\\n      <thead>\\n        <tr>\\n          {columns.map(col => (\\n            <th key={col.key} onClick={() => onSort(col.key)}>\\n              {col.label}\\n            </th>\\n          ))}\\n        </tr>\\n      </thead>\\n      <tbody>\\n        {data.map(row => (\\n          <tr key={row.id}>\\n            {columns.map(col => (\\n              <td key={col.key}>{row[col.key]}</td>\\n            ))}\\n          </tr>\\n        ))}\\n      </tbody>\\n    </table>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// UsersPage.jsx\\nfunction UsersPage() {\\n  const { processedData, page, setPage, sortBy, setSortBy, filter, setFilter } =\\n    useTable(usersData, { pageSize: 20 });\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input value={filter} onChange={e => setFilter(e.target.value)} />\\n      <Table\\n        data={processedData}\\n        onSort={setSortBy}\\n        columns={[\\n          { key: 'name', label: 'Name' },\\n          { key: 'email', label: 'Email' },\\n        ]}\\n      />\\n      <button onClick={() => setPage(page - 1)} disabled={page === 1}>Prev</button>\\n      <span>Page {page}</span>\\n      <button onClick={() => setPage(page + 1)}>Next</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2470,"For a cancellable useEffect with an asynchronous request, use the cleanup function returned from the effect. Inside it you can cancel the request through an AbortController, or set a flag in order to ignore the result after the unmount. That prevents an update of the state of an unmounted component and prevents memory leaks.","The problem of asynchronous requests in useEffect\\nWhen a component performs an asynchronous request inside a useEffect, the request can complete after the component has already unmounted. If you try to update the state (setState) at that moment, React will issue a warning about memory. To avoid that, you have to cancel the request or ignore its result when the effect is cleaned up.\\n\\nThe solution with an AbortController\\nThe modern way is to use an AbortController, which lets you cancel a fetch request. Create a controller inside the effect, pass its signal into the fetch, and call abort() in the cleanup.\\n\\nThe solution with a flag\\nIf you are not using fetch (axios or setTimeout, for example), you can set a flag that is checked before the state is updated.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse an AbortController for fetch requests — that is the standard, reliable way of cancelling them. For other asynchronous operations a flag will do. Always clean your effects up, in order to avoid memory leaks and errors in React applications.","useEffect(() => {\\n  const controller = new AbortController();\\n  const signal = controller.signal;\\n\\n  fetch('/api/data', { signal })\\n    .then(res => res.json())\\n    .then(data => setData(data))\\n    .catch(err => {\\n      if (err.name !== 'AbortError') console.error(err);\\n    });\\n\\n  return () => controller.abort();\\n}, []);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  let cancelled = false;\\n\\n  fetchData().then(data => {\\n    if (!cancelled) setData(data);\\n  });\\n\\n  return () => { cancelled = true; };\\n}, []);"],[2471,"Batching in React 18 is the automatic grouping of several setState calls into one repeat render. Previously batching worked only inside React's event handlers, and now it is switched on by default for every update, including promises, setTimeout and native events. That improves the performance by reducing the number of redundant renders. To switch batching off, flushSync is used.","What is the batching of state updates?\\nBatching is the mechanism by which React groups several \`setState\` (or \`useState\`) calls into one update, causing only one repeat render of the component. That is critically important for performance, since every render is a costly operation, involving the recomputation of the virtual DOM and the synchronization with the real DOM.\\n\\nHow did it work before?\\nBefore React 18 batching was applied only inside React's event handlers (\`onClick\`, for example). Outside those handlers — in promises, \`setTimeout\`, native events — every \`setState\` call caused a separate render. That led to redundant repaints and reduced performance.\\n\\nAutomatic batching in React 18\\nStarting with React 18, batching became automatic and works for every state update, independently of the context. That means that even if you call \`setState\` inside a \`setTimeout\`, a \`fetch\` or a \`Promise\`, React will group them into one render.\\n\\nHow do you switch batching off?\\nIn the rare cases where a render has to be forced after every update (for reading DOM properties, for example), you can use \`flushSync\` from \`react-dom\`:\\n\\nConclusion\\nAutomatic batching in React 18 is a performance improvement, which works out of the box and requires no additional configuration. It is worth applying in every project on React 18, since it reduces the number of renders and makes the application more responsive. The exceptions are the cases where a synchronous reading of the DOM is required after every update, but they are rare.","import { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  function handleClick() {\\n    // React 18: one render\\n    setCount(c => c + 1);\\n    setCount(c => c + 1);\\n    setCount(c => c + 1);\\n  }\\n\\n  function handleAsync() {\\n    setTimeout(() => {\\n      // React 18: one render (automatic batching)\\n      setCount(c => c + 1);\\n      setCount(c => c + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n  }\\n\\n  return <button onClick={handleClick}>{count}</button>;\\n}\\n\\nimport { flushSync } from 'react-dom';\\n\\nfunction handleClick() {\\n  flushSync(() => setCount(c => c + 1));\\n  flushSync(() => setCount(c => c + 1));\\n  // Two separate renders\\n}"],[2472,"No, it will not work. Arrow functions are not subject to hoisting, unlike ordinary functions. They behave like variables declared through const or let and are in the temporal dead zone until the moment of their initialization. A call before the declaration will therefore lead to a ReferenceError.","Why can an arrow function not be called before its declaration?\\nIn JavaScript there is a hoisting mechanism, which moves the declarations of variables and functions to the start of their scope. For arrow functions declared through const or let, however, hoisting works differently: they are not initialized until the line with the declaration runs. Until that moment the variable is in the temporal dead zone (TDZ).\\n\\nAn example with a React functional component\\n\\nIn this example the call of handleClick() happens before the handleClick variable has been initialized. JavaScript sees the declaration const handleClick, but it does not let you address it before the initialization has completed. As a result a ReferenceError arises.\\n\\nHow does this differ from ordinary functions?\\nOrdinary functions declared through a function declaration are hoisted completely, together with their body. They can be called before the line of the declaration:\\n\\nThe practical conclusion\\nIn React functional components arrow functions are convenient for event handlers, but they have to be declared before they are used. If a function has to be called before its declaration, use an ordinary function. In every other case arrow functions are preferable, because of the lexical this and the concise syntax.","function MyComponent() {\\n  // An error: Cannot access 'handleClick' before initialization\\n  handleClick();\\n\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    console.log('Clicked');\\n  };\\n\\n  return <div>Hello</div>;\\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n  handleClick(); // It works without errors\\n\\n  function handleClick() {\\n    console.log('Clicked');\\n  }\\n\\n  return <div>Hello</div>;\\n}"],[2473,"useMemo compares the dependencies by means of Object.is — that is, by reference. If a new object created on every render is passed into the array of dependencies, its reference will differ from the previous one. React considers that the dependencies have changed and recomputes the memoized value, which negates the memoization effect.","How useMemo works\\nThe useMemo hook in React takes a factory function and an array of dependencies. It remembers the result of the call of the function and returns it on the repeat renders, until the dependencies change. The comparison of the dependencies happens by means of Object.is, which means a strict comparison by reference for objects and arrays.\\n\\nThe problem with new objects\\nIf you pass into the array of dependencies an object created directly in the component's body, then on every render a new object with a new reference is created. For example:\\n\\nHere options is created afresh on every render, so useMemo sees a new reference and recomputes the value, ignoring the memoization.\\n\\nThe right solution\\nFor the memoization to work, you have to stabilize the reference to the object. That can be done by means of useMemo, or by moving the object outside the component:\\n\\nOr by means of a useMemo for the object itself:\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor effective memoization with useMemo, the references to the objects in the array of dependencies have to stay stable between renders. Otherwise the memoization loses its point and you get a recomputation on every render.","const MyComponent = () => {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const options = { threshold: 0.5 };\\n  const memoizedValue = useMemo(() => computeExpensiveValue(options), [options]);\\n  // ...\\n};\\n\\nconst OPTIONS = { threshold: 0.5 };\\nconst MyComponent = () => {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const memoizedValue = useMemo(() => computeExpensiveValue(OPTIONS), [OPTIONS]);\\n  // ...\\n};\\n\\nconst options = useMemo(() => ({ threshold: 0.5 }), []);\\nconst memoizedValue = useMemo(() => computeExpensiveValue(options), [options]);"],[2474,"Rather than passing the object as a whole, you have to pass its primitive properties individually. Instead of useMemo(() => fn(obj), [obj]), for example, use useMemo(() => fn(obj.a, obj.b), [obj.a, obj.b]). That guarantees useMemo will recompute the value only when those particular primitives change rather than when a new object with the same values is created.","The problem of passing an object in the dependencies\\nWhen you pass an object into useMemo's array of dependencies, React compares the references to the objects. If the object is created afresh on every render (even with the same values of its fields), useMemo will recompute the value every time, which negates the optimization.\\n\\nThe solution: passing primitives\\nRather than the object, pass its primitive properties individually. That lets React compare the values rather than the references.\\n\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at a component that filters a list on the basis of some parameters:\\n\\nWhen is this useful\\nThis approach matters especially when working with object props from parent components, with context or with the results of other hooks. It prevents unnecessary recomputations and improves the performance.\\n\\nConclusion: pass into useMemo's dependencies only the primitive values that really do affect the result. That makes the code more predictable and more efficient, especially in components with frequent re-renders.","// Bad: the object is created afresh every render\\nconst obj = { a: 1, b: 2 };\\nconst result = useMemo(() => compute(obj), [obj]);\\n\\n// Good: we pass the primitives\\nconst result = useMemo(() => compute(obj.a, obj.b), [obj.a, obj.b]);\\n\\nfunction FilteredList({ filters }) {\\n  // Bad: filters is an object, and may be a new reference\\n  const filtered = useMemo(() => {\\n    return items.filter(item => item.price > filters.minPrice);\\n  }, [filters]);\\n\\n  // Good: we pass only the primitives needed\\n  const filtered = useMemo(() => {\\n    return items.filter(item => item.price > filters.minPrice);\\n  }, [filters.minPrice]);\\n}"],[2475,"To avoid a degradation of the performance with infinite scroll, use the virtualization of the list (react-window or react-virtualized, for example), which renders only the visible elements. Also apply the lazy loading of the data, pagination on the server and the clean-up of unused DOM nodes. That reduces the load on the memory and speeds the rendering up.","The performance problem with infinite scroll\\nInfinite scroll adds new elements to the DOM as the page is scrolled. Without optimization the number of nodes grows, which leads to increased memory consumption, slower rendering and lag during the scrolling. The main task is to minimize the number of elements displayed at once.\\n\\nThe solution: the virtualization of the list\\nVirtualization (or windowing) renders only the elements that are in the visible area (the viewport) and replaces them during the scrolling. That lets you keep a fixed number of nodes in the DOM, independently of the overall size of the data.\\n\\nAn example with the react-window library:\\n\\nAdditional optimizations\\n\\n- The lazy loading of the data: load the new elements only when the end of the list is approached (the Intersection Observer).\\n- Pagination on the server: request the data in portions (offset/limit), so as not to load it all at once.\\n- Cleaning the resources up: remove the event handlers and unsubscribe from the subscriptions when the components unmount.\\n- Debouncing the scroll: limit the frequency with which the scroll handler is called, in order to avoid redundant repaints.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe virtualization of the list is the key method for maintaining high performance with infinite scroll. Apply it in combination with lazy loading and server-side pagination, in order to work with large sets of data in web applications.","import { FixedSizeList as List } from 'react-window';\\n\\nconst Row = ({ index, style }) => (\\n  <div style={style}>Item {index}</div>\\n);\\n\\nconst InfiniteList = ({ items }) => (\\n  <List\\n    height={400}\\n    itemCount={items.length}\\n    itemSize={50}\\n    width={300}\\n  >\\n    {Row}\\n  </List>\\n);"],[2476,"Logic with useEffect and fetch is worth moving into a custom hook when that logic is repeated in several components. That improves the readability of the code, makes testing easier and lets you manage the loading state, the errors and the data centrally. A custom hook encapsulates the side effects and makes the components cleaner.","When to move logic with useEffect and fetch into a custom hook\\nIn React components should concentrate on drawing the UI rather than on managing side effects. If you notice that one and the same pattern with \`useEffect\` and \`fetch\` is repeated in several components, that is a clear signal for moving the logic into a custom hook. That not only reduces the duplication of code but also makes the components more readable and more testable.\\n\\nAn example: without a custom hook\\n\\nAn example: with a custom hook\\n\\nWhen is this especially useful\\n\\n- The data-loading logic is used in several components (lists, detail pages, for example).\\n- You want to add the handling of errors, the cancellation of requests or caching centrally.\\n- A component becomes too big because the logic and the UI are mixed.\\n\\nConclusion: move your fetch and useEffect into a custom hook when you notice repeated code or want to improve the modularity and the testability of the application. That is standard practice in React development, which makes the code cleaner and more maintainable.","function UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch(\`/api/users/\${userId}\`)\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => {\\n        setUser(data);\\n        setLoading(false);\\n      });\\n  }, [userId]);\\n\\n  if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;\\n  return <div>{user.name}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nfunction useFetch(url) {\\n  const [data, setData] = useState(null);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch(url)\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => {\\n        setData(data);\\n        setLoading(false);\\n      });\\n  }, [url]);\\n\\n  return { data, loading };\\n}\\n\\nfunction UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  const { data: user, loading } = useFetch(\`/api/users/\${userId}\`);\\n  if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;\\n  return <div>{user.name}</div>;\\n}"],[2477,"Yes, a re-render will happen. React compares the previous and the new state by means of a strict comparison (===). If you pass in a new object, even with the same data, that is a new object in memory, so React considers the state changed and starts a re-render of the component.","Why does a re-render happen when a new object with the same data is passed?\\nIn React the \`useState\` function returns an array of two elements: the current state, and a function for updating it. When you call the update function with a new value, React compares the previous state with the new one by means of the strict equality operator (\`===\`). For the primitive types (numbers, strings, booleans) the comparison happens by value. For objects and arrays, by reference.\\n\\nIf you create a new object with the same fields and values, that is still a new object in memory, so the reference differs from the previous one. React sees that the reference has changed and starts a re-render of the component.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nOn every click on the button 'Re-render!' will be printed, since a new object is created every time.\\n\\nHow do you avoid redundant re-renders?\\nIf you want to avoid a re-render when the data is unchanged, you have to pass the very same reference to the object. You can hold the object in a variable outside the component, for example, or use \`useMemo\` for the memoization.\\n\\nIn that case a re-render will not happen, since the reference has not changed.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of referential identity matters for optimizing the performance of React applications. If you do not want to cause a re-render when the data is unchanged, always use one and the same reference to the object or the array.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction UserComponent() {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: 'Alice', age: 30 });\\n\\n  const updateUser = () => {\\n    // We create a new object with the same data\\n    setUser({ name: 'Alice', age: 30 });\\n  };\\n\\n  console.log('Re-render!');\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>{user.name}, {user.age}</p>\\n      <button onClick={updateUser}>Update</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nconst initialUser = { name: 'Alice', age: 30 };\\n\\nfunction UserComponent() {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(initialUser);\\n\\n  const updateUser = () => {\\n    // We pass the same reference\\n    setUser(initialUser);\\n  };\\n  // ...\\n}"],[2478,"Use React.memo to wrap the child component, so that it re-renders only when its props change. For callback functions apply useCallback, and for objects useMemo, so that the references stay stable. Also avoid passing new objects or functions on every render of the parent.","The problem of redundant re-renders\\nIn React, when a parent component's state or props change, all of its child components re-render by default. That can lead to redundant computation and reduced performance, especially if the child component is heavy or there are many of them.\\n\\nThe main methods of optimization\\n\\n- React.memo — a wrapper for a functional component, which compares the previous and the new props (shallowly) and skips the render if they have not changed.\\n- useCallback — a hook for memoizing functions. It returns the same reference to the function until the dependencies change.\\n- useMemo — a hook for memoizing values (objects, arrays). It lets you avoid creating new references on every render.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn the example \`Child\` is wrapped in \`React.memo\`, and \`handleClick\` is memoized through \`useCallback\`. When \`count\` changes, the parent re-renders, but \`Child\` does not render again, since the \`onClick\` prop has stayed the same.\\n\\nConclusion\\nApply \`React.memo\` to components that often receive the same props, and \`useCallback\`/\`useMemo\` to stabilize the references to functions and objects. That is especially useful in lists, forms and components with heavy drawing.","import React, { useState, useCallback, memo } from 'react';\\n\\nconst Child = memo(({ onClick }) => {\\n  console.log('Child render');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    console.log('clicked');\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n      <Child onClick={handleClick} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2479,"Suspense displays a fallback component while the data loads, while an Error Boundary catches the errors that arise in the child components, including errors during the loading. They work together: Suspense manages the loading state and the Error Boundary the error state. If an error arises during the loading, it is thrown up to the nearest Error Boundary, which displays the error UI. That lets you separate the loading logic from the handling of the errors.","How Suspense and an Error Boundary work together\\nSuspense and Error Boundary are two React mechanisms for managing asynchronous states. Suspense is responsible for displaying a fallback UI (a spinner, for example) while the data loads, while an Error Boundary intercepts the errors in the child components and shows a fallback error interface. They can be nested inside one another, in order to handle the different scenarios.\\n\\nAn example of using them together\\nLet us look at a component that loads data by means of Suspense. If the loading ends in an error, it is thrown up to the nearest Error Boundary.\\n\\nHere Suspense shows \\"Loading...\\" until DataComponent is ready. If an error happens during the loading, the ErrorBoundary will display \\"A loading error\\".\\n\\nThe order of the handling\\n\\n- Suspense intercepts the promise thrown by the component and shows the fallback.\\n- If the promise is rejected (an error), React throws the exception up the tree.\\n- The Error Boundary catches that exception and displays its own fallback.\\n\\nThus Suspense and Error Boundary do not conflict but complement one another: the first manages the loading state, the second the error state.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse Suspense for displaying the loading and an Error Boundary for handling the errors. That separation makes maintaining the code easier and makes the UI more predictable.","import React, { Suspense } from 'react';\\nimport { ErrorBoundary } from 'react-error-boundary';\\n\\nconst DataComponent = React.lazy(() => import('./DataComponent'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <ErrorBoundary fallback={<h2>A loading error</h2>}>\\n      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n        <DataComponent />\\n      </Suspense>\\n    </ErrorBoundary>\\n  );\\n}"],[2480,"To avoid a double call of fetch, you can use an external store of state — a global state manager (Redux, Zustand) or React Context, for example. Another approach is to cache the request's result in a module-level variable or by means of the React Query library, which deduplicates the requests automatically. You can also lift the state up to a common parent and pass the data down through props.","The problem of a double call of fetch\\nWhen one and the same custom hook performing a fetch is used in two different components on one page, each component initiates its own request when it mounts. That leads to duplicated network calls, which increases the load on the server and slows the application down.\\n\\nThe solution by means of an external store\\nOne way is to move the request's state into a global store — into Redux or React Context, for example. The hook will check whether the data is already in the store, and if it is, it will not perform a new request.\\n\\nThe solution by means of the React Query library\\nReact Query (TanStack Query) provides built-in deduplication of requests. If several components use one and the same query key, the library combines them into one call automatically.\\n\\nThe solution by means of a module-level cache\\nYou can create a simple cache in a module-level variable, which will hold the request's result. The hook checks the cache before performing the fetch.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of method depends on the application's complexity: for the simple cases a module-level cache will do, for the medium ones React Context, and for large projects with a great many requests React Query or Redux Toolkit Query.","// useData.js\\nimport { useContext } from 'react';\\nimport { DataContext } from './DataContext';\\n\\nexport function useData() {\\n  const { data, fetchData } = useContext(DataContext);\\n  if (!data) {\\n    fetchData(); // it is performed only once\\n  }\\n  return data;\\n}\\n\\nimport { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';\\n\\nfunction useData() {\\n  return useQuery(['data'], fetchData);\\n}\\n\\n// In any component:\\nconst { data } = useData();\\n\\nlet cache = null;\\n\\nexport function useData() {\\n  const [data, setData] = useState(cache);\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    if (!cache) {\\n      fetch('/api/data')\\n        .then(res => res.json())\\n        .then(result => {\\n          cache = result;\\n          setData(result);\\n        });\\n    }\\n  }, []);\\n  return data;\\n}"],[2481,"To compare objects by their fields in React, a shallow comparison is used. It checks whether the references to the object's fields have changed rather than their deep content. That is implemented in React.memo and shouldComponentUpdate. For the comparison to work properly, the objects have to be immutable — on a change a new object is created. Such an approach prevents redundant setState calls and re-renders.","The shallow comparison of objects in React\\nIn React, in order to optimize the performance, objects often have to be compared, so as to avoid unnecessary re-renders. The standard \`===\` operator compares the references rather than the content. React therefore uses a shallow comparison, which checks the equality of the references at every level of the object's fields.\\n\\nHow this works\\nA shallow comparison goes through the object's keys and compares the values by means of \`===\`. If at least one field has changed its reference, the comparison returns false and the component re-renders. That is efficient if the objects are immutable — on a change a new object with new references to the fields is created.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe application in React\\n\\n- \`React.memo\` uses a shallow comparison for functional components.\\n- \`shouldComponentUpdate\` in class components lets you implement your own comparison logic.\\n- \`useMemo\` and \`useCallback\` also rely on a shallow comparison of the dependencies.\\n\\nConclusion: a shallow comparison is the standard way of optimizing in React. It is efficient when the immutability of the data is observed, and it helps prevent redundant setState calls and re-renders, which matters especially in large applications.","const prevProps = { user: { name: 'John' } };\\nconst nextProps = { user: { name: 'John' } };\\n\\n// The shallow comparison\\nfunction shallowEqual(objA, objB) {\\n  if (Object.is(objA, objB)) return true;\\n  if (typeof objA !== 'object' || objA === null ||\\n      typeof objB !== 'object' || objB === null) {\\n    return false;\\n  }\\n  const keysA = Object.keys(objA);\\n  const keysB = Object.keys(objB);\\n  if (keysA.length !== keysB.length) return false;\\n  for (let i = 0; i < keysA.length; i++) {\\n    if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(objB, keysA[i]) ||\\n        !Object.is(objA[keysA[i]], objB[keysA[i]])) {\\n      return false;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return true;\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(shallowEqual(prevProps, nextProps)); // false, since user is a different object"],[2482,"useMemo memoizes the result of a function's computation and recomputes it only when the dependencies change. That prevents expensive computations on every render when a piece of state updates that does not affect the result. If you have the filtering of a large array, for example, useMemo guarantees it will be performed only when the array or the filtering condition changes.","What useMemo is, and what it is for\\nuseMemo is a React hook, which remembers (memoizes) the result of running a function and returns it on the repeat renders, until the dependencies stated change. That is especially useful when a component updates often because of changes of state but some computations should be performed only when particular data changes.\\n\\nHow this works\\nOn every render React runs the component's body. If there are heavy computations inside (sorting, filtering, complex mathematical operations, for example), they will be started again and again, even if the input data has not changed. useMemo solves that problem by caching the result until the dependencies change.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example a press of the button updates the count state, which causes a re-render of App and of ExpensiveList. Without useMemo the filtering would be performed every time, even if items and filter had not changed. With useMemo it is performed only when items or filter changes.\\n\\nWhen to apply it\\nuseMemo is worth using when you have expensive computations that depend on the props or the state and those computations should not be repeated on every render. You should not abuse it, however — for simple operations the overhead of the memoization can be greater than the benefit.\\n\\nConclusion: useMemo helps you optimize the performance of React components by preventing unnecessary repeat computations during frequent state updates, but apply it deliberately, only to operations that really are heavy.","import React, { useState, useMemo } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction ExpensiveList({ items, filter }) {\\n  // Without useMemo: the filtering is performed on every render\\n  // With useMemo: only when items or filter changes\\n  const filteredItems = useMemo(() => {\\n    console.log('Filtering...');\\n    return items.filter(item => item.includes(filter));\\n  }, [items, filter]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {filteredItems.map((item, index) => (\\n        <li key={index}>{item}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const items = ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry', 'date'];\\n  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('');\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>\\n      <input value={filter} onChange={e => setFilter(e.target.value)} />\\n      <ExpensiveList items={items} filter={filter} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2483,"React Server Components (RSC) run on the server and send finished HTML to the client, which reduces the size of the bundle and improves the performance. Unlike client components, they have no access to hooks, events, state or effects. RSC cannot use the browser APIs and do not support interactivity. Client components, on the contrary, work in the browser and can use all of React's capabilities, but they require JavaScript to be downloaded.","What are React Server Components?\\nReact Server Components (RSC) are components that run exclusively on the server during the rendering. They send the client finished HTML, without the need to download and run JavaScript for those components. That lets you reduce the size of the bundle considerably and speed the page's loading up, especially for static or rarely changing content.\\n\\nThe limitations compared with client components\\nRSC's main limitations are related to the fact that they have no access to the browser's environment. Here are the key differences:\\n- No state or effect hooks: RSC cannot use \`useState\`, \`useEffect\`, \`useReducer\` or the other hooks that depend on client state or on the lifecycle.\\n- No event handlers: you cannot add \`onClick\`, \`onChange\` or other events, since they require JavaScript on the client.\\n- No access to the browser APIs: RSC cannot use \`window\`, \`document\`, \`localStorage\` or other APIs.\\n- They do not support interactivity: RSC are intended only for rendering static content. For interactivity you have to use client components.\\n- They cannot be asynchronous in the classic sense: although RSC can be asynchronous (for loading data, for example), they do not support \`async/await\` in the same form as client components do.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\nAn example of a server component, which loads data and renders it:\\n\\nAn example of a client component with interactivity:\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact Server Components are useful for optimizing the performance by reducing the volume of JavaScript, but they should be applied only for static or server-side content. For interactive elements, be sure to use client components with the \`'use client'\` directive.","// ServerComponent.jsx (it runs on the server)\\nimport { getPosts } from './api';\\n\\nexport default async function ServerComponent() {\\n  const posts = await getPosts();\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {posts.map(post => (\\n        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// ClientComponent.jsx (it runs in the browser)\\n'use client';\\nimport { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nexport default function ClientComponent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>\\n      Clicked {count} times\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}"],[2484,"Caching the results of asynchronous requests on the client lets you avoid repeat requests to the server for one and the same data. A Map or WeakMap object is normally used, in which the key is the request's string and the value is a Promise or the result. On a new request the cache is checked first, and if the data is already there, the saved value is returned. That speeds the application up and reduces the load on the server.","The basic idea of client-side caching\\nCaching the results of asynchronous requests on the client is the technique in which the responses from the server are saved in the browser's memory, so that on a repeat request with the same parameters a new HTTP request is not sent. That is especially useful for data that changes rarely — lists of countries, a user's settings or search results, for example.\\n\\nHow this works\\nThe simplest way is to use a \`Map\` object, in which the key is a string representing the request (the URL plus the parameters, for example) and the value is a Promise or the data already obtained. When the request function is called, the presence of the key in the cache is checked first. If the key is there and the data is still current, the saved value is returned. If not, the real request is performed, the result is saved in the cache and returned.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\n\\nIn this example we cache the Promise itself, in order to avoid repeat requests even if several components request the same data at once.\\n\\nManaging the cache's lifetime\\nThe cache should not hold the data for ever. You can add a lifetime (TTL) for every item. You can hold a timestamp together with the data, for example, and check whether the cache has gone stale when it is read.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- In React applications, by means of the React Query or SWR libraries, which cache the requests automatically.\\n- In mobile applications, in order to reduce the traffic.\\n- In any SPA, in order to speed the repeat display of data up.\\n\\nConclusion: the client-side caching of asynchronous requests is a simple, efficient way of improving the performance and the user experience, especially when working with data that is updated infrequently. It is worth applying everywhere repeat requests to the same resources are possible.","const cache = new Map();\\n\\nasync function fetchWithCache(url) {\\n  if (cache.has(url)) {\\n    return cache.get(url);\\n  }\\n  const promise = fetch(url).then(res => res.json());\\n  cache.set(url, promise);\\n  return promise;\\n}\\n\\nfunction setWithExpiry(key, data, ttl) {\\n  const item = { data, expiry: Date.now() + ttl };\\n  cache.set(key, item);\\n}\\n\\nfunction getWithExpiry(key) {\\n  const item = cache.get(key);\\n  if (!item) return null;\\n  if (Date.now() > item.expiry) {\\n    cache.delete(key);\\n    return null;\\n  }\\n  return item.data;\\n}"],[2485,"When implementing an infinite feed, it matters to provide for the virtualization of the list, in order to avoid memory leaks and hangs. You have to implement pagination with a cursor or an offset, in order to load the data in portions. It is also worth adding a debounce on the scroll, the caching of the posts loaded, and the handling of loading errors with the ability to retry.","The main problems of an infinite feed\\nAn infinite news feed is a popular UX pattern, but it creates serious technical challenges. The main problem is the accumulation of DOM nodes during the scrolling, which leads to a collapse in the performance and to memory leaks. The second problem is managing the state: the data loaded, the scroll position and the real-time updates have to be synchronized.\\n\\nThe virtualization of the list\\nTo solve the performance problem, virtualization is used (react-window or react-virtualized, for example). It displays only the visible elements, replacing the invisible ones with empty placeholders of the right height. That lets you keep no more than 10–20 elements in the DOM, independently of the feed's length.\\n\\nPagination and the loading of the data\\nAn infinite feed should not load every post at once. Cursor-based pagination is used — the server returns the identifier of the last post, and the next request takes the data after it. That is more efficient than offset pagination, since it does not suffer from insertions and deletions in the middle of the list.\\n\\nManaging the state and the UX\\nIt matters to preserve the scroll position when the user returns to the feed (after viewing a post, for example). sessionStorage or Redux is used for that. You also have to add loading indicators, the handling of errors (with a \\"Retry\\" button) and a debounce on the scroll event, so as not to send requests too often.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn infinite feed requires a combination of virtualization, cursor-based pagination and competent state management. This approach is justified for social networks and news aggregators, where the content is updated often and the user expects smooth scrolling without delays.","import { FixedSizeList as List } from 'react-window';\\n\\nconst Feed = ({ items }) => (\\n  <List height={600} itemCount={items.length} itemSize={120} width=\\"100%\\">\\n    {({ index, style }) => (\\n      <div style={style}>\\n        <Post data={items[index]} />\\n      </div>\\n    )}\\n  </List>\\n);\\n\\n// An example of a request with a cursor\\nfetch(\`/api/posts?cursor=\${lastCursor}&limit=20\`)\\n  .then(res => res.json())\\n  .then(data => {\\n    setPosts(prev => [...prev, ...data.items]);\\n    setCursor(data.nextCursor);\\n  });"],[2486,"Snapshot testing is a method of automatic testing, which compares a component's or some data's current state with a saved reference snapshot. If the snapshots do not match, the test fails, signalling unexpected changes. That helps you reveal regressions in the UI or in the structure of the data quickly.","What is snapshot testing?\\nSnapshot testing is an approach to automatic checking in which a test creates a snapshot of a component's, an element tree's or some serialized data's current state and compares it with a reference snapshot saved earlier. If the snapshots match, the test passes; if not, the test fails, pointing to the changes.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nSnapshot testing is normally used together with frameworks such as Jest. On the first run of the test a file with the reference snapshot is created (.snap, for example). On the subsequent runs the test generates a new snapshot and compares it with the reference. If the developer changes the component deliberately, they have to update the reference snapshot (with the jest --updateSnapshot command, for example).\\n\\nAn example of the code (Jest + React)\\n\\nOn the first run the file __snapshots__/MyComponent.test.js.snap is created. On a repeat run Jest compares the output with that file.\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- Checking UI components for regressions after a refactor.\\n- Verifying serialized data (the JSON responses of an API, for example).\\n- Fast feedback when the structure of the components changes.\\n\\nConclusion\\nSnapshot testing is useful for the automatic detection of unexpected changes in the UI or in the data, especially in large projects with frequent changes. It does not replace unit tests of the logic, however, and it requires careful updating of the snapshots when changes are deliberate.","import renderer from 'react-test-renderer';\\nimport MyComponent from './MyComponent';\\n\\ntest('MyComponent renders correctly', () => {\\n  const tree = renderer.create(<MyComponent />).toJSON();\\n  expect(tree).toMatchSnapshot();\\n});"],[2487,"To pass a dynamic number of columns into a layout component, use props.children. That lets you nest any number of child elements, which will be displayed inside the component. A single CSS class is applied to the layout component's container, while the styling of the columns can be implemented through CSS Grid or Flexbox, where the child elements are distributed across the grid automatically.","Passing a dynamic number of columns through props.children\\nIn React, to create a layout component that can take an arbitrary number of columns, the special \`children\` prop is used. That prop contains all of the child elements passed between the component's opening and closing tags automatically. Such an approach lets you preserve a single CSS class for the container and implement the styling of the columns through CSS Grid or Flexbox.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\nLet us look at a \`ColumnsLayout\` component, which takes \`children\` and displays them as columns:\\n\\nThe CSS for that component uses Grid:\\n\\nNow any number of columns can be passed in:\\n\\nHow this works\\nThe \`children\` prop can be a single element, an array of elements or even a function (a render prop). In this case it is an array of three \`<div>\`s. CSS Grid distributes them across the columns automatically, thanks to the \`grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr))\` property. That means the columns will occupy at least 200px, and if there is more space, they will expand evenly.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUsing \`props.children\` is the standard, flexible way of creating layout components in React. It lets you pass a dynamic number of columns in without changing the component itself, while preserving a single CSS class for the container. This approach is especially useful when creating product cards, lists of items or any other repeated structures, where the number of items is unknown in advance.","// ColumnsLayout.jsx\\nimport './ColumnsLayout.css';\\n\\nfunction ColumnsLayout({ children }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\"columns-layout\\">\\n      {children}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nexport default ColumnsLayout;\\n\\n/* ColumnsLayout.css */\\n.columns-layout {\\n  display: grid;\\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));\\n  gap: 16px;\\n}\\n\\n// App.jsx\\nimport ColumnsLayout from './ColumnsLayout';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <ColumnsLayout>\\n      <div>Column 1</div>\\n      <div>Column 2</div>\\n      <div>Column 3</div>\\n    </ColumnsLayout>\\n  );\\n}"],[2488,"Virtual scroll is the technique in which only the visible part of a list is rendered rather than all of the elements. That is achieved by computing the elements' positions on the basis of the scroll and creating and removing DOM nodes dynamically. Such an approach reduces the load on the browser considerably when working with thousands of records.","What is virtual scroll?\\nVirtual scroll (or windowing) is a rendering technique in which only the elements of the list that are visible to the user at the given moment are present in the DOM. The remaining elements are replaced with empty placeholder blocks, which preserve the overall height of the scroll. That lets you work with lists of tens of thousands of elements without a loss of performance.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nThe main idea is to track the scroll position and compute which elements should be visible. For that, a container with a fixed height and overflow: auto is used. Inside it sits a scrollable block with an overall height equal to the height of all of the elements. The visible elements are rendered absolutely positioned inside that block.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation in React\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- Chats with thousands of messages\\n- Tables with a large number of rows\\n- Social networks' feeds\\n- Lists of files or logs\\n\\nConclusion: virtual scroll is worth applying when more than 1,000 elements have to be displayed in a list, in order to avoid the interface hanging and to ensure smooth scrolling.","import { useRef, useState, useCallback } from 'react';\\n\\nconst ITEM_HEIGHT = 50;\\nconst OVERSCAN = 3;\\n\\nfunction VirtualList({ items }) {\\n  const containerRef = useRef(null);\\n  const [scrollTop, setScrollTop] = useState(0);\\n\\n  const handleScroll = useCallback(() => {\\n    setScrollTop(containerRef.current.scrollTop);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const totalHeight = items.length * ITEM_HEIGHT;\\n  const startIndex = Math.max(0, Math.floor(scrollTop / ITEM_HEIGHT) - OVERSCAN);\\n  const endIndex = Math.min(items.length, Math.ceil((scrollTop + containerRef.current?.clientHeight) / ITEM_HEIGHT) + OVERSCAN);\\n\\n  const visibleItems = items.slice(startIndex, endIndex);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div ref={containerRef} onScroll={handleScroll} style={{ height: '400px', overflow: 'auto' }}>\\n      <div style={{ height: totalHeight, position: 'relative' }}>\\n        {visibleItems.map((item, index) => (\\n          <div key={item.id} style={{\\n            position: 'absolute',\\n            top: (startIndex + index) * ITEM_HEIGHT,\\n            height: ITEM_HEIGHT,\\n            width: '100%'\\n          }}>\\n            {item.text}\\n          </div>\\n        ))}\\n      </div>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2489,"Yes, when setState is called React creates a new state object, merging the current state with the data passed in. That is necessary for the detection of the changes and the repainting of the component to work properly. The old reference to the state object is replaced with a new one, which guarantees immutability.","How setState works in React\\nWhen you call setState, React does not mutate the current state object but creates a new one. That is the key principle of immutability, which lets React track the changes efficiently and decide when the component has to be re-rendered. setState performs a shallow merge of the object passed in with the current state, after which the reference to the state object is updated.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example setState creates a new object, copying the existing user property and replacing count. The old reference to this.state is no longer current.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- React uses a comparison of the references (reference equality) in order to determine the changes. If the state were mutated, React would not be able to detect the update reliably.\\n- Immutability makes debugging easier and the components' behaviour more predictable.\\n- It also lets you use PureComponent and React.memo for optimizing the performance.\\n\\nConclusion\\nsetState always creates a new reference to the state object, which is a fundamental requirement for React to work properly. Apply immutable updates, in order to avoid bugs and to ensure the rendering is predictable.","// The initial state\\nthis.state = { count: 0, user: 'Alice' };\\n\\n// The setState call\\nthis.setState({ count: 1 });\\n\\n// The new state: { count: 1, user: 'Alice' }\\n// The reference to the state object has changed"],[2490,"useDeferredValue is a React hook, which lets you defer the update of part of the UI while preserving the responsiveness of the interface. It takes a value and returns a deferred version of it, which is updated with a delay if there are higher-priority updates. That is useful for a search: the typing of the text stays smooth while the results are displayed with a small delay, without blocking the input.","What is useDeferredValue?\\nuseDeferredValue is a hook from React 18, which lets you defer the update of a particular part of the interface, so as not to block more urgent updates such as the typing of text. It works on the basis of the Concurrent Features concept, in which React can interrupt and resume the rendering.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nThe hook takes the current value (a search string, for example) and returns a deferred version of it. While the user types fast, React defers the update of the search results, in order to handle the changes in the input field first. When the typing slows down or stops, the deferred value is updated and the results are rendered.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere to apply it?\\nuseDeferredValue is ideal for scenarios in which there are expensive computations or rendering that depend on input that changes fast: a search, the filtering of lists, autocompletion. It replaces a manual debounce or throttle, providing built-in optimization.\\n\\nConclusion: use useDeferredValue when the responsiveness of the interface has to be preserved during frequent state updates, especially in search components, in order to avoid delays in the input and to improve the user experience.","import { useState, useDeferredValue, useMemo } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction SearchPage() {\\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');\\n  const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query);\\n\\n  const results = useMemo(() => {\\n    return fetchResults(deferredQuery);\\n  }, [deferredQuery]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />\\n      <ResultsList results={results} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2491,"React 19 introduces several key innovations: the new use() hook for the asynchronous loading of data, improved support for Server Components, Actions for making work with forms easier, and improvements in Concurrent Mode such as the automatic batching of state updates. The useOptimistic hook for optimistic updates of the UI has also appeared.","React 19's main innovations\\nReact 19 is a considerable update, which improves the performance, makes development easier and extends the framework's capabilities. The key changes include the new \`use()\` hook, improved support for server components, Actions for working with forms, and optimistic updates.\\n\\nThe new use() hook\\nThe \`use()\` hook lets you load data asynchronously directly inside a component, replacing the need for a \`useEffect\` for simple requests. It works with promises and with context, making the code simpler.\\n\\nServer Components and Actions\\nReact 19 improves the support for server components, letting you run code on the server and send only finished HTML. Actions are a new pattern for handling forms, which manages the loading state and the errors automatically.\\n\\nOptimistic updates\\nThe \`useOptimistic\` hook lets you update the UI instantly while the server handles the request, and roll the changes back in the event of an error.\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact 19 makes development easier, especially in asynchronous scenarios and when working with forms. These innovations are worth applying in new projects, in order to improve the performance and the readability of the code.","import { use } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  const user = use(fetchUser(userId));\\n  return <div>{user.name}</div>;\\n}\\n\\n'use server';\\n\\nexport async function submitForm(formData) {\\n  const name = formData.get('name');\\n  // handling the data\\n}\\n\\nconst [optimisticState, addOptimistic] = useOptimistic(state, (currentState, newValue) => {\\n  return { ...currentState, value: newValue };\\n});"],[2492,"When its value changes, React Context re-renders all of the consumer components, even if they use only part of the data. State managers such as Redux let you use selectors, which subscribe only to particular parts of the state. That prevents redundant re-renders and improves the performance.","The difference between a state manager and context\\nReact Context is the built-in mechanism for passing data down the component tree without props. It has a drawback, however: when the context's value changes, every component that uses that context re-renders, even if they do not use the part of the data that has changed. That can lead to performance problems in large applications.\\n\\nState managers such as Redux provide a more flexible approach. They let you create selectors — functions that extract only the part of the state that is needed. The components subscribe to the selector's result rather than to the whole state. Thanks to that, a re-render happens only when the particular part of the data the component depends on changes.\\n\\nAn example with React Context\\n\\nAn example with Redux and selectors\\n\\nIn Redux the selectors can be memoized (by means of createSelector from Reselect, for example), which optimizes the computation additionally. Context, by contrast, provides no built-in means of memoization or of targeted subscription.\\n\\nConclusion: use React Context for the simple cases, when the data changes rarely or the application is small. For complex state with frequent updates and a great many consumers, state managers with selectors suit better, since they provide finer control over the re-renders and the performance.","const ThemeContext = React.createContext();\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      <Header />\\n      <Content />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Header() {\\n  const { theme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  // When theme changes, both Header and Content will re-render\\n}\\n\\nimport { useSelector } from 'react-redux';\\n\\nfunction Header() {\\n  const theme = useSelector(state => state.theme);\\n  // The component will re-render only when state.theme changes\\n}"],[2493,"useDebounce is a custom hook in React, which delays the update of a value until the user stops typing. It helps you avoid redundant requests to the server on every press of a key. In a real-time search, for example, useDebounce waits for a pause of 300 ms before sending the request. That reduces the load on the server and improves the application's performance.","What is useDebounce?\\nuseDebounce is a custom hook in React, which implements the debounce technique. It lets you defer the running of a function or the update of a piece of state until a particular interval of time has passed since the last call. That is especially useful when working with a real-time search, where every character typed can initiate a request to the server.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen the user types text, useDebounce does not update the value immediately. Instead it starts a timer, which is reset on every new input. Only once the user has stopped typing for the time set (300 ms, for example) is the value updated. That prevents a great many requests being sent during fast typing.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\n\\nThe application in a search\\nIn a search component you can use useDebounce in order to delay the sending of the request:\\n\\nConclusion\\nuseDebounce is a simple, efficient way of optimizing search requests, reducing the number of API calls and improving the user experience. It is worth applying everywhere frequent events have to be handled, such as the typing of text, the resizing of the window or scrolling.","import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction useDebounce(value, delay) {\\n  const [debouncedValue, setDebouncedValue] = useState(value);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const handler = setTimeout(() => {\\n      setDebouncedValue(value);\\n    }, delay);\\n\\n    return () => {\\n      clearTimeout(handler);\\n    };\\n  }, [value, delay]);\\n\\n  return debouncedValue;\\n}\\n\\nexport default useDebounce;\\n\\nimport useDebounce from './useDebounce';\\n\\nfunction SearchComponent() {\\n  const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState('');\\n  const debouncedSearchTerm = useDebounce(searchTerm, 300);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    if (debouncedSearchTerm) {\\n      fetchResults(debouncedSearchTerm);\\n    }\\n  }, [debouncedSearchTerm]);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <input\\n      value={searchTerm}\\n      onChange={(e) => setSearchTerm(e.target.value)}\\n      placeholder=\\"Search...\\"\\n    />\\n  );\\n}"],[2494,"Moving a UI library out into a separate layer or package means that all of the interface's components are gathered in an isolated module, which does not depend directly on the business logic. That makes reuse, testing and the replacement of the UI framework easier. Such an approach is often implemented through a monorepo or a separate npm package.","What does moving a UI library out into a separate layer mean?\\nIt is an architectural decision in which all of the user interface's components (buttons, forms, modal windows, tables) are separated out into a self-contained module or package. Such a module contains no business logic but is responsible only for the display and the interaction with the user. It can be published as a separate npm package or sit in a separate directory of a monorepo.\\n\\nWhat is it for?\\n\\n- Reuse — the same components can be used in different projects or in different parts of one application.\\n- Testing — UI components are easier to test in isolation, without a dependency on server data.\\n- Replacing the framework — if a move from React to Vue is required, it is enough to replace only the UI layer, without touching the business logic.\\n- Standardization — a single set of components ensures a consistent appearance and behaviour.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\nSuppose we have a React application. We create a separate \`@mycompany/ui\` package:\\n\\nThen in the main application we import that component:\\n\\nAt the same time \`@mycompany/ui\` has no access to the state management or to the API requests — it receives all of its data through props.\\n\\nConclusion\\nMoving a UI library out into a separate layer or package is a practice that improves the maintainability and the scalability of frontend projects. It is especially useful in large applications with several teams, or when several products with a single design have to be supported.","// packages/ui/src/Button.tsx\\nimport React from 'react';\\n\\ninterface ButtonProps {\\n  label: string;\\n  onClick: () => void;\\n  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';\\n}\\n\\nexport const Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({ label, onClick, variant = 'primary' }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <button className={\`btn btn-\${variant}\`} onClick={onClick}>\\n      {label}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// apps/main/src/App.tsx\\nimport { Button } from '@mycompany/ui';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <Button label=\\"Click me\\" onClick={() => alert('Hello!')} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2495,"A useEffect with dependencies unites componentDidMount, componentDidUpdate and componentWillUnmount. When you pass in an array of dependencies, the effect is started on mounting and on every update of the dependencies stated. That lets you replace several lifecycle methods with one hook.","The relation of useEffect to the lifecycle methods\\nThe \`useEffect\` hook in functional React components replaces several lifecycle methods of class components. When you pass in an array of dependencies, \`useEffect\` behaves like a combination of \`componentDidMount\` and \`componentDidUpdate\`.\\n\\nHow this works\\nWhen the component mounts, the effect runs once. On every update of any of the dependencies stated, the effect is started again. If the dependencies are empty (\`[]\`), the effect runs only on mounting and unmounting.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse a \`useEffect\` with dependencies for performing side effects that have to respond to changes of particular props or state. That makes the code simpler and more predictable than several lifecycle methods.","import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n\\n  // The analogue of componentDidMount + componentDidUpdate for userId\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch(\`/api/users/\${userId}\`)\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => setUser(data));\\n\\n    // The analogue of componentWillUnmount\\n    return () => {\\n      console.log('Cleanup for userId:', userId);\\n    };\\n  }, [userId]); // The dependency on userId\\n\\n  return <div>{user ? user.name : 'Loading...'}</div>;\\n}"],[2496,"If an element's type has changed, React considers that the old and the new tree are completely different. It will delete the old node and all of its child elements, then create a new node from scratch. That leads to the component's state being reset and to a remount.","What happens when an element's type changes\\nIn React the reconciliation process compares the virtual DOM trees. If an element's type has changed (a \`<div>\` has become a \`<span>\`, for example, or one component has been replaced with another), React does not try to update the existing node. Instead it deletes the old node and all of its child elements completely, after which it creates a new node from scratch.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nThis behaviour guarantees that the old component's state and side effects are not carried over into the new one. If you had a \`<Counter />\` component with an internal counter state, for example, and you replaced it with a \`<Timer />\`, React will destroy the Counter and create the Timer afresh. That prevents the unexpected behaviour in which the old state stays in the new component.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nOn a press of the button the element's type changes from Counter to Timer. React will delete the Counter (calling the cleanup in its useEffect) and mount the Timer afresh.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA change of an element's type is a signal to React to recreate the node completely. That is useful when you have to guarantee that the old component does not affect the new one — when switching between different widgets or pages, for example.","function App() {\\n  const [isCounter, setIsCounter] = useState(true);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => setIsCounter(!isCounter)}>\\n        Toggle\\n      </button>\\n      {isCounter ? <Counter /> : <Timer />}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2497,"useEffect is asynchronous. It does not block the rendering of the component but is started after the browser has painted the changes. That lets you perform side effects such as requests to an API or subscriptions without slowing the interface down.","The asynchronous nature of useEffect\\nThe \`useEffect\` hook in React is asynchronous. That means it is not performed during the rendering of the component but is started after the browser has finished painting the changes on the screen. Such behaviour lets you avoid blocking the user interface while performing long operations such as requests to a server or work with timers.\\n\\nHow this works\\nReact uses a mechanism similar to a \`setTimeout\` with a zero delay. When a component renders, React schedules the running of the effect but does not start it at once. The effect goes into the queue of microtasks and is performed once all of the synchronous operations have completed. That guarantees the user sees the current interface before the side actions begin.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe practical application\\nuseEffect's asynchrony is especially useful for:\\n- Loading data from a server when a component mounts.\\n- Subscribing to events or to a WebSocket.\\n- Setting timers and intervals up.\\n- Working with DOM elements after the rendering.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse \`useEffect\` for every side effect that should not block the rendering. That is the standard way of integrating asynchronous operations into React components, which ensures a smooth user experience.","import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Example() {\\n  const [data, setData] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // This code will run asynchronously after the rendering\\n    fetch('/api/data')\\n      .then(response => response.json())\\n      .then(json => setData(json));\\n  }, []); // An empty array of dependencies — the effect will run once\\n\\n  return <div>{data ? data.name : 'Loading...'}</div>;\\n}"],[2498,"If useEffect returns a function, React will call it when the component unmounts or before the effect is started again. That is called the cleanup function. It is used for cancelling subscriptions, timers or requests, in order to avoid memory leaks. You can clear an interval or unsubscribe from a WebSocket, for example.","What is the cleanup function in useEffect?\\nWhen you pass a function into useEffect, it runs after every render (if the dependencies have changed). If that function returns another function, React saves it and calls it when the component unmounts or before the next run of the effect. That is called the cleanup function.\\n\\nWhat is it for?\\nWithout the clean-up, side effects can accumulate: timers keep ticking, subscriptions stay active, and requests can update an unmounted component. That leads to memory leaks and errors. The cleanup function guarantees the resources are released.\\n\\nAn example with a timer\\n\\nIn this example the interval is created once (an empty array of dependencies). When the component is removed from the DOM, React calls the cleanup function, which stops the timer.\\n\\nAn example with a subscription\\n\\nHere, when dataSource changes, the cleanup is performed first (unsubscribing from the old source), then the new effect.\\n\\nConclusion: use the cleanup function in useEffect for any long-lived operations (timers, subscriptions, event handlers), in order to avoid memory leaks and errors in React applications.","import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Timer() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const id = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    // cleanup: we clear the interval on unmounting\\n    return () => clearInterval(id);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return <div>{count}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const subscription = dataSource.subscribe();\\n  return () => subscription.unsubscribe();\\n}, [dataSource]);"],[2499,"For a new back-office application I would choose React with TypeScript for the frontend, Node.js with Express or Nest.js for the backend, and PostgreSQL as the database. For state management — Redux Toolkit or Zustand; for the styles — CSS Modules or Tailwind CSS. That will provide scalability, typing and ease of maintenance.","The choice of the technology stack\\nFor a new back-office application it matters to ensure stability, scalability and convenience in maintenance. I would choose React with TypeScript on the frontend — that gives strict typing, reduces the number of bugs and makes refactoring easier. On the backend, Node.js with Express or Nest.js, since that lets you use one language across the whole stack, speeding the development up. For the database, PostgreSQL, since it is reliable and supports complex queries and transactions, which is typical of a back office.\\n\\nState management\\nFor the state management I would use Redux Toolkit or Zustand. Redux Toolkit suits large applications with a great many pieces of global state, since it is structured and has built-in support for asynchronous actions through createAsyncThunk. Zustand is a lighter alternative, if the application is not too complex. An example of setting Redux Toolkit up:\\n\\nThe approach to CSS\\nFor the styles I would choose CSS Modules or Tailwind CSS. CSS Modules isolate the styles by component, preventing conflicts of names, which matters in large teams. Tailwind CSS speeds the development up thanks to its utility classes, but it can make the readability harder in complex layouts. An example with CSS Modules:\\n\\nConclusion\\nSuch an approach provides modularity, typing and ease of maintenance, which is critical for back-office applications, where reliability and the ability to introduce changes quickly matter.","import { configureStore, createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\n\\nconst userSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'user',\\n  initialState: { name: '', role: '' },\\n  reducers: {\\n    setUser: (state, action) => {\\n      state.name = action.payload.name;\\n      state.role = action.payload.role;\\n    },\\n  },\\n});\\n\\nexport const { setUser } = userSlice.actions;\\n\\nexport const store = configureStore({\\n  reducer: { user: userSlice.reducer },\\n});\\n\\n/* Button.module.css */\\n.button {\\n  background-color: #007bff;\\n  color: white;\\n  padding: 10px 20px;\\n  border: none;\\n  border-radius: 4px;\\n}\\n\\n// Button.tsx\\nimport styles from './Button.module.css';\\n\\nexport const Button = () => <button className={styles.button}>Click</button>;"],[2500,"When a callback is used inside a map, every render creates a new function. If that callback is passed into a child component wrapped in React.memo, the memoization does not fire, since the reference to the function changes. That causes a redundant re-render of the child component. To solve it, useCallback is used, or the function is moved outside the component.","The problem of redundant re-renders when a callback is used in a map\\nWhen you use a callback inside the map method (or similar structures) in a React component, a new function is created every time it renders. That is normal JavaScript behaviour, but it can cause undesirable re-renders of the child components.\\n\\nHow this works\\nLet us look at an example: you have a list of items, and for every item you pass a callback function in. If the child component is wrapped in React.memo, it compares the previous and the new props. Since the function is created afresh on every render, the reference to it changes and React.memo considers that the props have changed, causing a re-render.\\n\\nThe solution with useCallback\\nUse the useCallback hook, in order to memoize the callback. It will return the very same function until the dependencies change. That prevents redundant re-renders.\\n\\nThe alternative approaches\\n\\n- Move the callback outside the component, if it does not depend on the props or the state.\\n- Use useMemo in order to memoize the array of functions.\\n- Pass the item's identifier in rather than a callback, and handle the event in the parent.\\n\\nConclusion: use useCallback for stable references to the functions passed into child components, especially when working with large lists or with components wrapped in React.memo. That improves the performance and prevents redundant re-renders.","// An example of the problem\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const items = ['a', 'b', 'c'];\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      {items.map(item => (\\n        <Child key={item} onClick={() => handleClick(item)} />\\n      ))}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// The fixed example\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const items = ['a', 'b', 'c'];\\n  const handleClick = useCallback((item) => {\\n    console.log(item);\\n  }, []);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      {items.map(item => (\\n        <Child key={item} onClick={handleClick} />\\n      ))}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"]]`);export{e as default};